Here's a complete Appwrite Function in Node.js that handles a JSON body with a `name` field and returns a personalized greeting:

```javascript
// src/main.js
export default async ({ req, res, log, error }) => {
    // Only allow POST requests for this endpoint
    if (req.method !== 'POST') {
        return res.json(
            { error: 'Method not allowed. Use POST.' },
            405
        );
    }

    // Parse the JSON body (req.bodyJson is the parsed object)
    const data = req.bodyJson;

    // Validate the "name" field
    if (!data?.name || typeof data.name !== 'string') {
        error('Missing or invalid "name" field');
        return res.json(
            { error: 'A "name" field (string) is required.' },
            400
        );
    }

    log(`Greeting user: ${data.name}`);

    // Return the personalized greeting
    return res.json({
        message: `Hello, ${data.name}! Welcome to Appwrite.`
    });
};
```

### Key points about the structure

**Function signature** — The entry point file (e.g. `src/main.js`) must `export default` an `async` function that destructures the context object:

| Property | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `req` | The incoming request |
| `res` | Helper to build the response |
| `log` | Logs info to the execution console |
| `error` | Logs errors to the execution console |

**Reading the body:**
- `req.bodyJson` — the **parsed** JSON object (cleanest for JSON payloads)
- `req.body` — the raw string body (parse manually with `JSON.parse` if you prefer)

**Response format** — Always `return` a response helper. `res.json(data, statusCode)` automatically sets the `Content-Type: application/json` header. Other options include `res.text()`, `res.empty()`, `res.redirect()`, and `res.send()`.

### Example request/response

Request body:
```json
{ "name": "Ada" }
```

Response:
```json
{ "message": "Hello, Ada! Welcome to Appwrite." }
```

### `package.json`

Make sure your `package.json` declares ES modules so `export default` works:

```json
{
  "type": "module",
  "main": "src/main.js"
}
```

When configuring the function in Appwrite, set the **entry point** to `src/main.js` and choose a Node.js runtime (e.g. `node-18.0`). If you don't use `"type": "module"`, switch to CommonJS syntax instead: `module.exports = async ({ req, res, log, error }) => { ... }`.

Here's complete code using the Appwrite Web SDK to create a bucket, upload a file, and get a preview URL—with permission configuration at each step.

> **Important note:** Creating a **bucket** is a server-only operation (it requires an API key and the `node-appwrite` SDK). The Web SDK (`appwrite`) **cannot** create buckets. So I've split this into two parts:
> 1. **Server-side** (`node-appwrite`) — create the bucket
> 2. **Client-side** (`appwrite`, Web SDK) — upload the file and get the preview URL

---

## 1. Create the bucket (server-side — required)

```typescript
// server.ts — run on your backend (Node.js)
import { Client, Storage, Permission, Role, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const adminClient = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY); // API key with buckets.write scope

const storage = new Storage(adminClient);

const bucket = await storage.createBucket({
    bucketId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'Images',
    // Bucket-level permissions — define default access for all files in the bucket
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.any()),               // anyone can read files
        Permission.create(Role.users()),           // any logged-in user can upload
        Permission.update(Role.users()),
        Permission.delete(Role.users()),
    ],
    fileSecurity: true,                              // enable per-file permission overrides
    enabled: true,
    maximumFileSize: 10 * 1024 * 1024,               // 10 MB
    allowedFileExtensions: ['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'webp', 'gif'],
});

console.log('Bucket created:', bucket.$id);
```

> `fileSecurity: true` lets the per-file permissions you set during upload take effect. If `false`, only bucket-level permissions apply.

---

## 2. Upload a file & get a preview URL (client-side — Web SDK)

```typescript
// app.ts — runs in the browser
import { Client, Storage, Permission, Role, ID } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const storage = new Storage(client);

const BUCKET_ID = '[BUCKET_ID]'; // the $id from step 1

async function uploadAndPreview(userId: string) {
    // Get the selected file from an <input type="file" id="file-input">
    const input = document.getElementById('file-input') as HTMLInputElement;
    const selectedFile = input.files![0];

    // Upload with per-file permissions
    const file = await storage.createFile({
        bucketId: BUCKET_ID,
        fileId: ID.unique(),
        file: selectedFile,
        permissions: [
            Permission.read(Role.any()),          // public read access
            Permission.update(Role.user(userId)), // only the owner can modify
            Permission.delete(Role.user(userId)), // only the owner can delete
        ],
    });

    console.log('Uploaded file ID:', file.$id);

    // Get a resized/optimized preview URL for the image
    const previewUrl = storage.getFilePreview({
        bucketId: BUCKET_ID,
        fileId: file.$id,
        width: 400,
        height: 400,
        // Optional transforms:
        // gravity: ImageGravity.Center,
        // quality: 80,
        // output: ImageFormat.Webp,
    });

    console.log('Preview URL:', previewUrl);

    // Use it directly in an <img>
    const img = document.createElement('img');
    img.src = previewUrl.toString();
    document.body.appendChild(img);

    return previewUrl;
}
```

---

### Key points

| Operation | SDK | Why |
|-----------|-----|-----|
| Create bucket | `node-appwrite` (server) | Requires an API key; not available in the Web SDK |
| Upload file | `appwrite` (Web) | User-context upload from a file input |
| Get preview URL | `appwrite` (Web) | `getFilePreview` returns a URL synchronously (no `await`) |

**Permission tips:**
- `getFilePreview` only works on **image** files (jpg, png, gif, webp, etc.).
- For the preview to load in a browser, the file needs `Permission.read(Role.any())` — otherwise the user must be authenticated and have read access.
- Per-file permissions (set during upload) only apply if the bucket has `fileSecurity: true`.

If you're working entirely client-side and the bucket already exists (created via the Appwrite Console or CLI), you only need step 2.

Here's code that subscribes to both table row changes and file uploads simultaneously, handling each event type differently:

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

const DATABASE_ID = '[DATABASE_ID]';
const TABLE_ID = '[TABLE_ID]';
const BUCKET_ID = '[BUCKET_ID]';

// Subscribe to BOTH channels in a single subscription
const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb(DATABASE_ID).table(TABLE_ID).row(),
        Channel.bucket(BUCKET_ID).file(),
    ],
    (response) => {
        // Each event string looks like:
        //   'tablesdb.[DB].tables.[TABLE].rows.[ROW].create'
        //   'buckets.[BUCKET].files.[FILE].create'
        const events = response.events;

        // --- Route based on which resource the event belongs to ---

        // Table row events
        if (events.some((e) => e.includes('.rows.'))) {
            handleRowEvent(events, response.payload);
            return;
        }

        // File events
        if (events.some((e) => e.includes('.files.'))) {
            handleFileEvent(events, response.payload);
            return;
        }
    }
);

// ---- Handler for table row changes ----
function handleRowEvent(events: string[], payload: any) {
    if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.create'))) {
        console.log('Row created:', payload.$id, payload);
    } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.update'))) {
        console.log('Row updated:', payload.$id, payload);
    } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.delete'))) {
        console.log('Row deleted:', payload.$id);
    }
}

// ---- Handler for file uploads / changes ----
function handleFileEvent(events: string[], payload: any) {
    if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.create'))) {
        console.log('File uploaded:', payload.$id, payload.name, `${payload.sizeOriginal} bytes`);
    } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.update'))) {
        console.log('File updated:', payload.$id, payload.name);
    } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.delete'))) {
        console.log('File deleted:', payload.$id);
    }
}

// ---- Clean up when no longer needed ----
// await subscription.close();
```

### How it works

- **`realtime.subscribe([...channels], callback)`** accepts an array of channels, so a single subscription listens to both the table rows and the bucket files at once.
- The **callback's `response.events`** array contains event strings whose suffix (`.create`, `.update`, `.delete`) tells you the action, while the path segment (`.rows.` vs `.files.`) tells you the resource type.
- I route on resource type first, then branch on the specific action so each event type is handled differently.
- Calling **`subscription.close()`** unsubscribes from all channels in that subscription.

### Notes
- `Channel.tablesdb(db).table(id).row()` (no argument) targets *all* rows in the table; pass a row ID to scope to a single row.
- `Channel.bucket(id).file()` (no argument) targets *all* files in the bucket; pass a file ID to scope to a single file.
- The file payload (`Models.File`) exposes useful fields like `name`, `sizeOriginal`, `mimeType`, and `bucketId`.

If you'd prefer two **separate** subscriptions (e.g., so you can independently close one), let me know and I can show that variant too.

Here's a single `realtime.subscribe()` call listening to both a table's rows and a bucket's files:

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(),
        Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file(),
    ],
    (response) => {
        console.log(response.channels); // which channel matched
        console.log(response.events);   // event(s) that fired
        console.log(response.payload);  // affected row or file
    }
);

// Later, to stop listening:
// await subscription.close();
```

## The exact channel strings

When the `Channel` helpers are resolved, they produce these literal strings:

| Resource | Channel string |
|----------|----------------|
| All rows in a table | `tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows` |
| All files in a bucket | `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files` |

So if you prefer, you can pass the raw strings directly instead of the builder:

```typescript
const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        'tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows',
        'buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files',
    ],
    (response) => { /* ... */ }
);
```

**Notes:**
- Substitute your real IDs for the bracketed placeholders.
- To narrow to a single resource, append the ID: `tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows.[ROW_ID]` or `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files.[FILE_ID]`.
- The callback fires for events on **either** channel; use `response.channels` or `response.events` to distinguish which resource triggered it.

# Email/Password Auth with the Appwrite Web SDK

## 1. Install the SDK

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

## 2. Set up the client

```ts
// src/lib/appwrite.ts
import { Client, Account, ID } from 'appwrite';

export const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // e.g. 'fra.cloud.appwrite.io/v1'
  .setProject('<YOUR_PROJECT_ID>');

export const account = new Account(client);
export { ID };
```

> Put `<REGION>` and `<YOUR_PROJECT_ID>` in env vars (e.g. `VITE_APPWRITE_ENDPOINT`, `VITE_APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID`) for any non-trivial app.

## 3. Sign up (create account)

```ts
import { AppwriteException } from 'appwrite';
import { account, ID } from './lib/appwrite';

export async function signUp(email: string, password: string, name: string) {
  try {
    const user = await account.create({
      userId: ID.unique(),
      email,
      password,
      name,
    });
    // Auto-login after signup (optional)
    await signIn(email, password);
    return user;
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof AppwriteException) {
      // 409 = user already exists, 400 = weak password/validation, etc.
      throw new Error(err.message);
    }
    throw err;
  }
}
```

## 4. Sign in (create email/password session)

```ts
import { AppwriteException } from 'appwrite';
import { account } from './lib/appwrite';

export async function signIn(email: string, password: string) {
  try {
    const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
      email,
      password,
    });
    return session; // { $id, userId, secret, expire, ... }
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof AppwriteException) {
      // 401 = invalid credentials, 429 = rate limited
      throw new Error(err.message);
    }
    throw err;
  }
}
```

## 5. Get the current user / sign out

```ts
import { account } from './lib/appwrite';

export async function getCurrentUser() {
  try {
    return await account.get();
  } catch {
    return null; // no active session
  }
}

export async function signOut() {
  await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
}
```

## 6. Putting it together in a component

```tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { signUp, signIn } from './auth';

export default function AuthForm() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
  const [password, setPassword] = useState('');
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  const [mode, setMode] = useState<'signup' | 'login'>('signup');
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

  async function handleSubmit(e: React.FormEvent) {
    e.preventDefault();
    setError(null);
    try {
      if (mode === 'signup') {
        await signUp(email, password, name);
      } else {
        await signIn(email, password);
      }
      // Navigate to your protected page
    } catch (err) {
      setError((err as Error).message);
    }
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      {mode === 'signup' && (
        <input
          type="text"
          placeholder="Name"
          value={name}
          onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}
          required
        />
      )}
      <input
        type="email"
        placeholder="Email"
        value={email}
        onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)}
        required
      />
      <input
        type="password"
        placeholder="Password (min 8 chars)"
        minLength={8}
        value={password}
        onChange={(e) => setPassword(e.target.value)}
        required
      />
      <button type="submit">
        {mode === 'signup' ? 'Create account' : 'Sign in'}
      </button>
      <button type="button" onClick={() => setMode(mode === 'signup' ? 'login' : 'signup')}>
        Switch to {mode === 'signup' ? 'login' : 'signup'}
      </button>
      {error && <p style={{ color: 'red' }}>{error}</p>}
    </form>
  );
}
```

## Key gotchas

- **Email/password auth must be enabled** in your Appwrite Console → *Auth → Settings*.
- **`createEmailPasswordSession`** is the dedicated method — don't reuse `createSession` (that's for OAuth tokens). On Appwrite Cloud, sessions are limited to 100 per user (oldest is pruned on the 101st).
- **Password rules** (enforced server-side): minimum 8 chars by default. If you get a `400` on signup, check the console's auth settings.
- **Use `ID.unique()`** for `userId` unless you have a reason to supply your own (e.g. matching an external system).
- For SSR (Next.js, SvelteKit, etc.) you can't use the web SDK directly — see the SSR section in the docs and use `node-appwrite` with cookies named `a_session_<PROJECT_ID>`.

# Appwrite Web SDK — Create Database, Table, and Row

> **Important:** Creating databases and tables are **server-side only** operations that require an API key. Use the server SDK (`node-appwrite`) for setup, or call the web SDK from a backend with an API key. Client-side apps (browser/mobile) can only create rows, not databases or tables.

Here's a complete, runnable script that uses the `appwrite` package (Web SDK) to do everything end-to-end. If you want to run this in the browser, skip the `create` calls and pre-create the database/table on the server.

## Installation

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

## Code

```ts
import { Client, TablesDB, ID, Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // e.g. 'https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1'
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>'); // Required for createDatabase / createTable

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

async function main() {
    // 1) Create the database (server-side only)
    const database = await tablesDB.create({
        databaseId: ID.unique(),
        name: 'TaskTracker',
    });
    const databaseId = database.$id;
    console.log('Created database:', databaseId);

    // 2) Create the "tasks" table with columns
    const table = await tablesDB.createTable({
        databaseId,
        tableId: ID.unique(),
        name: 'tasks',
        columns: [
            { key: 'title',     type: 'varchar',  size: 255, required: true  },
            { key: 'completed', type: 'boolean',            required: false, default: false },
            { key: 'dueDate',   type: 'datetime',           required: false },
        ],
    });
    const tableId = table.$id;
    console.log('Created table:', tableId);

    // 3) Insert a row
    const row = await tablesDB.createRow({
        databaseId,
        tableId,
        rowId: ID.unique(),
        data: {
            title: 'Write Appwrite quickstart',
            completed: false,
            dueDate: new Date('2026-02-01T17:00:00.000Z').toISOString(),
        },
        permissions: [
            Permission.read(Role.any()),
            Permission.update(Role.any()),
            Permission.delete(Role.any()),
        ],
    });

    console.log('Created row:', row);
}

main().catch((err) => console.error(err));
```

## Column types used

| Column    | Type       | Notes                                                      |
|-----------|------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
| `title`     | `varchar`  | Inline string, max 16,383 chars; fully indexable.          |
| `completed` | `boolean`  | `true` / `false`.                                          |
| `dueDate`   | `datetime` | ISO 8601 string, e.g. `"2026-02-01T17:00:00.000Z"`.        |

## Browser-only variant (rows only)

If you want to do the row insert from the browser using a logged-in user, drop the API key and use a session instead. The `create` and `createTable` calls must be removed (do them once on the server first):

```ts
import { Client, TablesDB, ID, Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const row = await tablesDB.createRow({
    databaseId: '<DATABASE_ID>',
    tableId: '<TABLE_ID>',
    rowId: ID.unique(),
    data: {
        title: 'Buy groceries',
        completed: false,
        dueDate: '2026-02-15T10:00:00.000Z',
    },
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.user('<USER_ID>')),
        Permission.update(Role.user('<USER_ID>')),
        Permission.delete(Role.user('<USER_ID>')),
    ],
});
```

## Tips

- **Idempotency:** If you re-run the script, capture the generated `databaseId` and `tableId` and reuse them instead of calling `create` again.
- **Permissions:** Rows inherit table-level permissions by default. Only set `permissions` on `createRow` when you need per-row overrides.
- **Object-params style:** This SDK uses named parameters (`{ databaseId, tableId, ... }`) — preferred over positional arguments for readability.

# Querying Rows in Appwrite TablesDB with the `Query` Class

The `Query` class (imported alongside `TablesDB`) builds query strings that you pass in the `queries` array to `tablesDB.listRows()`. Each method returns a query object — combine as many as you need; by default they are joined with **AND**.

## Setup

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite';
// Server-side: import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const response = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        // build queries here
    ]
});

response.documents; // row objects
response.total;     // total count
```

---

## 1. Filtering

### Equality & inequality
```typescript
Query.equal('status', 'active')        // status == 'active'
Query.notEqual('status', 'archived')   // status != 'archived'

// Pass an array for IN queries
Query.equal('role', ['admin', 'editor'])  // role IN ('admin', 'editor')
```

### Numeric / date comparisons
```typescript
Query.lessThan('age', 30)
Query.lessThanEqual('age', 30)
Query.greaterThan('age', 18)
Query.greaterThanEqual('age', 18)
Query.between('age', 18, 65)          // 18 <= age <= 65
```

### Null checks
```typescript
Query.isNull('deletedAt')
Query.isNotNull('avatarUrl')
```

### String matching
```typescript
Query.startsWith('name', 'Al')         // "Alice", "Alex", ...
Query.endsWith('email', '@appwrite.io')
Query.contains('tags', 'featured')     // works on string contains or array contains
```

### Full-text search
```typescript
Query.search('body', 'appwrite tutorial') // requires a full-text index on the column
```

### Logical composition
```typescript
// Explicit AND (queries are already AND-joined by default)
Query.and([
    Query.greaterThan('age', 18),
    Query.lessThan('age', 65)
])

// OR
Query.or([
    Query.equal('role', 'admin'),
    Query.equal('role', 'editor')
])
```

**Example — combine filters:**
```typescript
const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'articles',
    queries: [
        Query.equal('status', 'published'),
        Query.greaterThanEqual('views', 100),
        Query.or([
            Query.startsWith('title', 'Appwrite'),
            Query.contains('tags', 'featured')
        ])
    ]
});
```

---

## 2. Sorting

```typescript
Query.orderAsc('createdAt')
Query.orderDesc('views')
```

**Example — newest published first:**
```typescript
const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'articles',
    queries: [
        Query.equal('status', 'published'),
        Query.orderDesc('createdAt')
    ]
});
```

> The order of sort fields determines priority — earlier sorts win over later ones. To sort by multiple fields, add multiple `orderAsc`/`orderDesc` calls in the desired priority order.

---

## 3. Limiting

`Query.limit()` caps the number of rows returned in a single response.

```typescript
Query.limit(25) // default is 25, max is 100
```

**Example — top 10 most-viewed:**
```typescript
const top = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'articles',
    queries: [
        Query.equal('status', 'published'),
        Query.orderDesc('views'),
        Query.limit(10)
    ]
});
```

**Selecting specific fields** (also a kind of "limit" on the response payload):
```typescript
queries: [Query.select(['title', 'summary', 'author'])]
```

---

## 4. Pagination

There are two strategies: **offset** (simple, but slow on large datasets) and **cursor** (recommended for large datasets).

### Offset-based
```typescript
const page1 = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'articles',
    queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.offset(0)]
});

const page2 = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'articles',
    queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.offset(25)]
});
```

`response.total` contains the total number of rows matching your filters, which you can use to compute total pages.

### Cursor-based (preferred for large datasets)
Cursor pagination uses the `$id` of the last/first row from the previous page — it's stable and fast even with millions of rows.

```typescript
// First page
const first = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'articles',
    queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.orderDesc('$createdAt')]
});

// Next page — pass the last row's ID
const lastId = first.documents[first.documents.length - 1].$id;
const second = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'articles',
    queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'), Query.cursorAfter(lastId)]
});

// Walk backwards with cursorBefore
const previous = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'articles',
    queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'), Query.cursorBefore(firstId)]
});
```

---

## Putting it all together

A realistic search/list page query that filters, sorts, paginates, and shapes the response:

```typescript
async function searchArticles({ search, page = 0, perPage = 20 }) {
    const queries = [
        Query.equal('status', 'published'),
    ];

    if (search) queries.push(Query.search('title', search));

    queries.push(
        Query.orderDesc('publishedAt'),
        Query.limit(perPage),
        Query.offset(page * perPage),
        Query.select(['$id', 'title', 'summary', 'author', 'publishedAt'])
    );

    const res = await tablesDB.listRows({
        databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
        tableId: 'articles',
        queries
    });

    return {
        rows: res.documents,
        total: res.total,
        hasMore: (page + 1) * perPage < res.total
    };
}
```

**Tips:**
- The default `Query.limit()` is **25**, the maximum is **100**. For more, use pagination.
- You can combine as many queries as you need — there's no artificial cap, but more filters can affect index usage.
- For frequently-queried fields, make sure your columns have appropriate indexes configured in the Appwrite Console.
- Prefer **cursor pagination** (`cursorAfter` / `cursorBefore`) over `offset` for any dataset that may grow large.

# Filtering and Sorting Rows with Query

Appwrite's `Query` helper lets you compose filter, sort, and pagination conditions that you pass as an array to `listRows`. Multiple queries are combined with **AND** by default, so you can just stack them.

## The Three Queries You Need

```typescript
Query.greaterThan('price', 100)   // Filter:  field > 100
Query.orderDesc('price')         // Sort:    field descending
Query.limit(20)                  // Page:    return at most 20 rows
```

## Full Example

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite';        // or 'node-appwrite' server-side

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.greaterThan('price', 100),
        Query.orderDesc('price'),
        Query.limit(20),
    ],
});

console.log(results.documents); // up to 20 rows, highest price first
console.log(results.total);     // total matching rows (ignores limit)
```

## How It Works

- **`Query.greaterThan('price', 100)`** — keeps only rows whose `price` is strictly greater than 100. Use `Query.greaterThanEqual(...)` if you want `>= 100`.
- **`Query.orderDesc('price')`** — sorts the matching rows by `price` from largest to smallest. For the opposite order, use `Query.orderAsc('price')`.
- **`Query.limit(20)`** — caps the result set at 20 rows. The hard maximum is 100; if you need more, paginate with `Query.offset()` or, for large datasets, prefer `Query.cursorAfter('[ROW_ID]')` for stable, efficient paging.
- **Order doesn't matter** within the `queries` array — Appwrite applies filters first, then sorts, then paginates.

## Common Variations

```typescript
// Include 100: field >= 100
Query.greaterThanEqual('price', 100)

// Range filter
Query.greaterThan('price', 100), Query.lessThan('price', 500)

// Paginate the next page
Query.limit(20), Query.offset(20)

// Cursor-based pagination (more efficient for large tables)
Query.limit(20), Query.cursorAfter(lastRowIdFromPreviousPage)
```

## Tip: Make It Type-Safe

For full TypeScript inference on the returned rows, pass a generic interface to `listRows<T>`:

```typescript
interface Product {
    name: string;
    price: number;
}

const results = await tablesDB.listRows<Product>({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [Query.greaterThan('price', 100), Query.orderDesc('price'), Query.limit(20)],
});

results.documents[0].price; // typed as number
```

> **Note:** For consistent performance, ensure the column you're filtering/sorting on has an index configured in your table settings. Sorting on unindexed columns works but can be slow on large tables.

```js
// src/main.js
export default async function ({ req, res, log, error }) {
  try {
    // req.body is automatically parsed when Content-Type is application/json
    const { name } = req.body;

    if (!name || typeof name !== "string") {
      return res.json(
        {
          ok: false,
          message: 'Missing or invalid "name" field in request body.',
        },
        400,
      );
    }

    log(`Greeting requested for: ${name}`);

    return res.json({
      ok: true,
      message: `Hello, ${name}! Welcome to Appwrite Functions.`,
      name,
    });
  } catch (err) {
    error("Failed to handle greeting request: " + err.message);
    return res.json(
      { ok: false, message: "Internal server error." },
      500,
    );
  }
}
```

### How it works

Appwrite Functions in Node.js export a default async function that receives a single context object with these properties:

| Property | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `req` | The incoming request. `req.body` is auto-parsed for `application/json`, `req.bodyRaw` gives the raw string, and `req.query` / `req.headers` / `req.method` / `req.path` are also available. |
| `res` | The response object. Use `res.json(payload, statusCode)` to return JSON, plus `res.text()`, `res.send()`, `res.empty()`, `res.redirect()`, `res.binary()`. |
| `log(...)` | Writes an info-level log visible in the execution log. |
| `error(...)` | Writes an error-level log. |

`res.json()` accepts `(payload, statusCode)` where `statusCode` defaults to `200`, which is why the error branches pass `400` and `500`.

### `package.json` (optional, in the function folder)

```json
{
  "type": "module",
  "main": "src/main.js"
}
```

The `"type": "module"` setting lets you use the `export default` syntax. If you prefer CommonJS, use:

```js
// src/main.js (CommonJS variant)
module.exports = async function ({ req, res, log, error }) {
  // ...same body as above
};
```

### Deploy it

From your `appwrite.config.json` add the function definition:

```json
{
  "functions": [
    {
      "$id": "greeting",
      "name": "Greeting",
      "runtime": "node-22",
      "entrypoint": "src/main.js",
      "path": "functions/greeting",
      "commands": "npm install"
    }
  ]
}
```

Then run:

```bash
appwrite push functions --function-id greeting --activate
```

### Invoke it

```bash
appwrite functions create-execution \
  --function-id greeting \
  --body '{"name":"Ada"}'
```

Response:

```json
{
  "ok": true,
  "message": "Hello, Ada! Welcome to Appwrite Functions.",
  "name": "Ada"
}
```

A few notes:
- You don't need any SDK dependency to read `req.body` — Appwrite parses JSON for you.
- For binary or streaming responses, use `res.binary()` or `res.send()` with a buffer/stream.
- For scheduled or event-driven invocations, `req.body` will contain the Appwrite event payload; the same handler works as long as you validate the input.

# Appwrite Web SDK: Bucket, Upload & Image Preview

Creating a bucket is a **server-side admin operation** (requires an API key), while file uploads and previews are typically done **client-side**. Here's a complete solution using both:

## 1. Create the Bucket (Server-side / Admin)

```typescript
// server.ts — uses the server SDK with an API key
import { Client, Storage, ID, Permission, Role } from 'node-appwrite';

const adminClient = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID!)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY!);

const storage = new Storage(adminClient);

const bucket = await storage.createBucket({
    bucketId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'User Avatars',
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.any()),        // public read so preview URLs work
        Permission.create(Role.users()),    // any logged-in user can upload
        Permission.update(Role.users()),
        Permission.delete(Role.users()),
    ],
    fileSecurity: true,                    // enable per-file permissions
    enabled: true,
    maximumFileSize: 10 * 1024 * 1024,     // 10 MB
    allowedFileExtensions: ['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'gif', 'webp'],
});

console.log('Bucket created:', bucket.$id);
```

## 2. Upload a File & Get Preview URL (Client-side)

```typescript
// client.ts — uses the Web SDK in the browser
import { Client, Storage, ID, Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const storage = new Storage(client);

async function uploadImage(bucketId: string, userId: string) {
    // Pick file from <input type="file" id="file-input" accept="image/*">
    const fileInput = document.getElementById('file-input') as HTMLInputElement;
    if (!fileInput.files?.[0]) throw new Error('No file selected');

    // Upload with per-file permissions
    const file = await storage.createFile({
        bucketId,
        fileId: ID.unique(),
        file: fileInput.files[0],
        permissions: [
            Permission.read(Role.any()),              // public preview
            Permission.update(Role.user(userId)),     // owner can update
            Permission.delete(Role.user(userId)),     // owner can delete
        ],
    });

    // Generate preview URL (returns a URL object — usable in <img src>)
    const preview = storage.getFilePreview({
        bucketId,
        fileId: file.$id,
        width: 400,                                  // resize
        height: 400,
        gravity: 'center',                           // 'center' | 'top-left' | etc.
        quality: 80,
        borderRadius: 8,                             // optional rounded crop
        output: 'jpg',                               // 'jpg' | 'png' | 'webp' | 'gif'
    });

    return { file, previewUrl: preview.toString() };
}

// Usage
const { file, previewUrl } = await uploadImage('<BUCKET_ID>', '<USER_ID>');

document.querySelector('img')!.src = previewUrl;
console.log('File ID:', file.$id);
```

## Key Points

| Concern | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Bucket creation** | Server-side only — must use `node-appwrite` with an API key |
| **Bucket-level permissions** | Set once at creation; default for all files inside |
| **File-level permissions** | Override per-file — requires `fileSecurity: true` on the bucket |
| **`Permission.read(Role.any())`** | Required on either the bucket OR the file for `getFilePreview` to work publicly |
| **Preview URL** | Returned synchronously by `getFilePreview()` — no network call. Pipe it directly into `<img src>`, `fetch()`, etc. |
| **Image processing** | `width`, `height`, `quality`, `gravity`, `borderRadius`, and `output` are all optional |

## Permissions Cheat-Sheet

```typescript
// Common patterns
Permission.read(Role.any())                          // public, including guests
Permission.read(Role.users())                        // any authenticated user
Permission.read(Role.user(userId))                   // specific user
Permission.read(Role.team(teamId))                   // any team member
Permission.read(Role.team(teamId, 'editor'))         // members with a specific role

Permission.write(Role.user(userId))                  // create + update + delete
Permission.update(Role.user(userId))                 // update only
Permission.delete(Role.user(userId))                 // delete only
```

> ⚠️ **Common mistake:** Setting `Permission.read(Role.any())` only on individual files but forgetting to enable `fileSecurity: true` on the bucket — the file-level permissions will be ignored.

# Appwrite Storage: `getFileView()` vs `getFileDownload()` vs `getFilePreview()`

All three methods generate a **URL string** (not the file data itself) that you assign to an `<img>`, `<a>`, `window.open()`, etc. The key differences are in **how the file is served** and **what transformations apply**.

## 1. `storage.getFileView()`

Returns a URL that serves the file **inline** (in the browser) with `Content-Disposition: inline`. Works for **any file type** — PDFs, videos, audio, images, etc. No transformations are applied.

```typescript
const result = storage.getFileView({
    bucketId: '[BUCKET_ID]',
    fileId: '[FILE_ID]'
});
// -> 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/storage/buckets/.../files/.../view'
```

**Use when:** You want users to *view* a file in the browser tab without forcing a download. For example, opening a PDF invoice, playing a video, or displaying a file's content directly in an iframe.

## 2. `storage.getFileDownload()`

Returns a URL that serves the file with `Content-Disposition: attachment`, **forcing the browser to download** it. Works for **any file type**. No transformations.

```typescript
const result = storage.getFileDownload({
    bucketId: '[BUCKET_ID]',
    fileId: '[FILE_ID]'
});
// -> 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/storage/buckets/.../files/.../download'
```

**Use when:** You want the user to save a copy locally — e.g., a "Download Report" button, exporting a CSV, downloading a ZIP.

> Note: In the browser SDK this returns a `URL` object; on the server SDK you typically fetch the bytes directly.

## 3. `storage.getFilePreview()`

Returns a URL for a **transformed image preview**. Only works for **image files** (PNG, JPG, WebP, etc.) and supports manipulation parameters like `width`, `height`, `gravity`, `quality`, `border`, `opacity`, `rotation`, `background`, and `output` format.

```typescript
const preview = storage.getFilePreview({
    bucketId: '[BUCKET_ID]',
    fileId: '[FILE_ID]',
    width: 300,
    height: 300,
    gravity: 'center',
    quality: 80,
    output: 'webp'
});
// -> 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/storage/buckets/.../files/.../preview?width=300&...'
```

**Use when:** You need a **resized/optimized image variant** — e.g., avatar thumbnails in a user list, card cover images, or any place where loading the full-resolution original would be wasteful.

## Quick Comparison

| Method | File types | Transformations | Browser behavior |
|---|---|---|---|
| `getFileView()` | Any | ❌ None | Display inline |
| `getFileDownload()` | Any | ❌ None | Force download |
| `getFilePreview()` | Images only | ✅ Resize, crop, quality, format, etc. | Display inline (as image) |

## Practical Examples

```typescript
// User avatar thumbnail in a list
<img src={storage.getFilePreview({
    bucketId: 'avatars',
    fileId: user.avatarId,
    width: 64, height: 64, gravity: 'face'
}).toString()} />

// "View PDF" button
<a href={storage.getFileView({
    bucketId: 'documents',
    fileId: invoice.fileId
}).toString()} target="_blank">View Invoice</a>

// "Download CSV" button
<a href={storage.getFileDownload({
    bucketId: 'exports',
    fileId: export.fileId
}).toString()}>Download Report</a>
```

**Rule of thumb:**
- Need a resized image? → `getFilePreview`
- Want the original file shown in the browser? → `getFileView`
- Want the original file saved to disk? → `getFileDownload`

# Subscribing to Realtime Table Rows and File Uploads

The Appwrite Web SDK lets you subscribe to multiple channels in a single `subscribe()` call, then branch on `response.events` to handle each kind of change. The event strings follow a predictable pattern (`tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.<action>` and `buckets.*.files.*.<action>`), which makes routing easy.

## Install

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

## Full example

```ts
import { Client, Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

// Subscribe to a table's rows AND a bucket's files in one call.
// Each response includes `events`, `payload`, `channels`, and `timestamp`.
const channels = [
    Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(), // all rows in the table
    Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file(),                       // all files in the bucket
];

const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(channels, (response) => {
    // Every event in Appwrite realtime is namespaced. Branching on the
    // first event is sufficient because the SDK delivers one logical
    // change per callback invocation.
    const event = response.events[0];

    if (event?.startsWith('tablesdb.')) {
        handleRowEvent(event, response.payload);
    } else if (event?.startsWith('buckets.')) {
        handleFileEvent(event, response.payload);
    } else {
        console.warn('Unhandled event:', event);
    }
});

function handleRowEvent(event: string, payload: any) {
    if (event.endsWith('.create')) {
        // e.g. "tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.create"
        console.log('🆕 Row created:', payload.$id, payload);
        // Example: prepend to a list, show a toast, etc.
    } else if (event.endsWith('.update')) {
        // e.g. "tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.update"
        console.log('✏️  Row updated:', payload.$id, payload);
        // Example: patch the row in a local cache by $id
    } else if (event.endsWith('.delete')) {
        // e.g. "tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.delete"
        console.log('🗑️  Row deleted:', payload.$id);
        // Example: remove from local state
    }
}

function handleFileEvent(event: string, payload: any) {
    if (event.endsWith('.create')) {
        // e.g. "buckets.*.files.*.create"
        console.log('📤 File uploaded:', payload.name, payload.$id);
        // Example: show progress, refresh gallery, generate a preview URL
        // const preview = storage.getFilePreview({ bucketId: '[BUCKET_ID]', fileId: payload.$id, width: 300, height: 300 });
    } else if (event.endsWith('.update')) {
        console.log('📝 File metadata updated:', payload.$id, payload);
    } else if (event.endsWith('.delete')) {
        console.log('🗑️  File deleted:', payload.$id);
    }
}

// Later — when the user navigates away or the component unmounts:
await subscription.close();
```

## How it works

**Channel selection.** `Channel.tablesdb(db).table(t).row()` listens to every row in that table. To watch a single row instead, call `.row('[ROW_ID]')`. For storage, `.file()` covers the whole bucket, and `.file('[FILE_ID]')` targets one file.

**Multiple channels, one callback.** Pass an array of channels to a single `subscribe()` call. The callback fires once per change, and `response.channels` tells you which channel(s) matched. Branching on `response.events[0]` is the standard way to route — every event in Appwrite is namespaced (`tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.<action>` and `buckets.*.files.*.<action>`), so a `startsWith` check is enough to dispatch.

**The payload.** For row events, `payload` is the affected `Models.Document` (with `$id`, `$createdAt`, `$updatedAt`, plus your columns). For file events, it's the `Models.File` metadata object (with `$id`, `name`, `mimeType`, `sizeOriginal`, `buckets` IDs, etc.) — not the file bytes.

**Cleanup.** `subscription.close()` removes the underlying WebSocket subscription. Call it when the component unmounts or the user leaves the page so you don't leak listeners.

**Tip:** if you want to be more selective, you can also subscribe to a specific row (`Channel.tablesdb(db).table(t).row('[ROW_ID]')`) or a specific file (`Channel.bucket(b).file('[FILE_ID]')`) to avoid being woken up by unrelated changes.

# Single `realtime.subscribe()` for TablesDB rows + Storage files

You pass an **array of channels** to one `subscribe()` call. Use the `Channel` builder to construct the channel strings safely (it stringifies to the exact format Appwrite expects).

```ts
import { Client, Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(),
        Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file(),
    ],
    (response) => {
        console.log(response.events);   // e.g. ['tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.create']
        console.log(response.payload);  // the affected row or file
    }
);

// Later:
await subscription.close();
```

## Exact channel strings (raw form)

| Builder expression | Underlying channel string |
|---|---|
| `Channel.tablesdb('DB_ID').table('TABLE_ID').row()` | `tablesdb.DB_ID.tables.TABLE_ID.rows` |
| `Channel.bucket('BUCKET_ID').file()` | `buckets.BUCKET_ID.files` |

## What you'll receive

A single callback fires for events on **either** channel. Distinguish them via `response.channels` (the matched channels) or `response.events`, e.g.:

- `tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.create` / `.update` / `.delete` — row events
- `buckets.*.files.*.create` / `.update` / `.delete` — file events

> 💡 You can also subscribe to a **specific** row or file by passing an ID:
> `Channel.tablesdb('DB_ID').table('TABLE_ID').row('ROW_ID')` → `tablesdb.DB_ID.tables.TABLE_ID.rows.ROW_ID`
> `Channel.bucket('BUCKET_ID').file('FILE_ID')` → `buckets.BUCKET_ID.files.FILE_ID`

# Email/Password Auth with the Appwrite Web SDK

Here's a complete implementation covering client setup, signup, login, plus a couple of common extras (getting the current user and logging out):

## 1. Install the SDK

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

## 2. Set Up the Client

```typescript
// appwrite.ts
import { Client, Account, ID } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // e.g. https://fra.cloud.appwrite.io/v1
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');                          // Your project ID from the Appwrite console

export const account = new Account(client);
export { ID };
```

## 3. Signup (Create an Account)

```typescript
import { account, ID } from './appwrite';

async function signup(email: string, password: string, name: string) {
    const user = await account.create({
        userId: ID.unique(),   // auto-generate a unique user ID
        email,
        password,
        name,
    });
    return user;
}
```

## 4. Login (Create a Session)

```typescript
import { account } from './appwrite';

async function login(email: string, password: string) {
    const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
        email,
        password,
    });
    return session;
}
```

## 5. Putting It Together (with error handling)

```typescript
import { AppwriteException } from 'appwrite';
import { account, ID } from './appwrite';

async function registerAndLogin(email: string, password: string, name: string) {
    try {
        // 1. Create the account
        await account.create({
            userId: ID.unique(),
            email,
            password,
            name,
        });

        // 2. Log the user in (creating an account does NOT create a session)
        await account.createEmailPasswordSession({ email, password });

        // 3. Fetch the logged-in user
        const user = await account.get();
        console.log('Logged in as:', user.name);
        return user;
    } catch (err) {
        if (err instanceof AppwriteException) {
            console.error(`Auth error (${err.code}): ${err.message}`);
            // e.g. 409 = email already exists, 401 = invalid credentials
        }
        throw err;
    }
}

// Logout when needed
async function logout() {
    await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
}
```

## Key Points

- **Signup doesn't log the user in** — `account.create()` only creates the user. You must call `createEmailPasswordSession()` afterward to start a session.
- **Sessions are persisted automatically** — the Web SDK stores the session in a cookie/localStorage, so subsequent calls like `account.get()` are authenticated without extra work.
- **Password requirements** — passwords must be at least 8 characters.
- **Common errors**: `409` means the email is already registered; `401` means invalid credentials on login.

# Appwrite Sessions Explained

A **session** in Appwrite represents an authenticated user's connection to your project. When a user logs in, Appwrite creates a session object containing metadata like the device, OS, IP, location, provider used, and an expiration date. The session secret is stored as a secure cookie (on web) or in persistent storage (on mobile), and the SDK automatically attaches it to subsequent requests.

## Creating Sessions

Sessions are created through any of Appwrite's authentication flows:

**Email/password login:**
```typescript
const account = new Account(client);
const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
    email: 'user@example.com',
    password: 'password123'
});
```

**OAuth2 (web):**
```typescript
account.createOAuth2Session({
    provider: OAuthProvider.Github,
    success: 'https://example.com/success',
    failure: 'https://example.com/fail'
});
```

**Token-based flows** (OAuth on mobile/SSR, magic URL, email OTP, phone OTP): these first issue a token, then you exchange the `userId` + `secret` for a session:
```typescript
const session = await account.createSession({ userId, secret });
```

**Anonymous sessions** — for guest users who can be converted to full accounts later:
```typescript
await account.createAnonymousSession();
```

**Server-side (SSR):** Use the server SDK with an admin client to create the session, then store `session.secret` yourself in an `httpOnly` cookie named `a_session_<PROJECT_ID>`, and pass it back via `client.setSession(secret)` on each request.

## Managing Sessions

A user can be logged in on multiple devices simultaneously — each device gets its own session. You can inspect and manage them:

```typescript
// Get the current session (or pass a specific session ID)
const current = await account.getSession({ sessionId: 'current' });

// List all active sessions for the user (all devices)
const sessions = await account.listSessions();

// Extend/refresh the current session (e.g., refresh OAuth tokens)
await account.updateSession({ sessionId: 'current' });

// Get the currently logged-in user
const user = await account.get();
```

The special `'current'` keyword always refers to the session making the request. Session objects expose useful fields like `provider`, `clientName`, `osName`, `ip`, `countryName`, and `expire`.

On the server side, the `Users` service lets admins manage any user's sessions (`users.listSessions()`, `users.deleteSessions()`, `users.createSession()` for custom token auth).

## Deleting Sessions (Logout)

```typescript
// Log out of the current device
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });

// Log out a specific session (e.g., from a "manage devices" UI)
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: '[SESSION_ID]' });

// Log out everywhere — deletes ALL sessions for the user
await account.deleteSessions();
```

Deleting all sessions is a common security measure after a password change or suspected account compromise.

## Session Limits & Expiration

- **Maximum sessions per user:** Appwrite enforces a configurable limit (default **10** sessions per user, configurable up to 100 in the Console under **Auth → Security**). When the limit is reached, **the oldest session is automatically deleted** to make room for the new one — logins never fail due to the limit.
- **Session length:** Sessions expire after a configurable duration (default **1 year**). You can change this in the Console under **Auth → Security → Session length**. Calling `updateSession()` extends the session.
- **Personal data in sessions:** You can also configure whether sessions expire when a user changes their password, depending on your security posture.

**Key takeaway:** Sessions are device-scoped, stateful credentials managed server-side by Appwrite — unlike JWTs (which Appwrite also offers via `account.createJWT()` for short-lived, 15-minute stateless tokens that piggyback on an active session).

Here's the code using the current **TablesDB** API (which replaces the deprecated `Databases` class). One important note first: **creating databases and tables requires an API key**, so those operations must run server-side with `node-appwrite`. Inserting rows can be done from the Web SDK in the browser.

## 1. Setup script (server-side, `node-appwrite`)

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

// Create the database
const db = await tablesDB.create({
    databaseId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'Task Manager',
});

// Create the "tasks" table with columns defined inline
const table = await tablesDB.createTable({
    databaseId: db.$id,
    tableId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'tasks',
    columns: [
        { key: 'title',     type: 'varchar',  size: 255, required: true  },
        { key: 'completed', type: 'boolean',             required: false },
        { key: 'dueDate',   type: 'datetime',            required: false },
    ],
});

console.log('Database:', db.$id, 'Table:', table.$id);
```

> `varchar` is the right choice for `title` — it's stored inline and fully indexable (up to size 768). The legacy `string` type is deprecated.

## 2. Insert a row (client-side, Web SDK `appwrite`)

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, ID, Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const row = await tablesDB.createRow({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    rowId: ID.unique(),
    data: {
        title: 'Finish project report',
        completed: false,
        dueDate: '2025-07-15T17:00:00.000Z', // ISO 8601 for datetime columns
    },
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),
        Permission.update(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),
        Permission.delete(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),
    ],
});

console.log('Created row:', row.$id);
```

Key points:

- **Object-params style** (`{ databaseId: ... }`) is the current recommended calling convention.
- **Datetime values** are passed as ISO 8601 strings.
- **Permissions matter**: without row-level permissions (or table-level permissions configured in the console), no user — including the creator — can read the row back. For client-side inserts, also make sure the table grants the `create` permission to authenticated users (e.g., `Role.users()`) in its settings.

Here's a complete Appwrite Function in Node.js that handles a JSON body with a `name` field:

```javascript
// src/main.js
export default async ({ req, res, log, error }) => {
    // Only accept POST requests with a JSON body
    if (req.method !== 'POST') {
        return res.json(
            { error: 'Method not allowed. Please use POST.' },
            405
        );
    }

    // req.bodyJson is the parsed JSON body
    // (undefined if the body isn't valid JSON)
    const data = req.bodyJson;

    if (!data?.name || typeof data.name !== 'string') {
        error('Missing or invalid "name" field in request body');
        return res.json(
            { error: 'A "name" field (string) is required.' },
            400
        );
    }

    log(`Greeting requested for: ${data.name}`);

    return res.json({
        greeting: `Hello, ${data.name}! Welcome to Appwrite Functions. 👋`
    });
};
```

### Key points about the function signature

- **Export**: The entry point must `export default` an async function.
- **Context object**: It receives a single destructured context object with:
  - `req` — the request: `req.bodyJson` (parsed JSON), `req.body` (raw string), `req.method`, `req.path`, `req.query`, `req.headers`
  - `res` — the response builder: `res.json(obj, status?, headers?)`, `res.text()`, `res.empty()`, `res.redirect()`, `res.send()`
  - `log` / `error` — use these instead of `console.log` so output appears in the Appwrite execution logs.
- **Return**: You must **return** one of the `res.*` methods — they build the response rather than sending it imperatively.

### Testing it

You can invoke it via the SDK:

```javascript
const execution = await functions.createExecution({
    functionId: '[FUNCTION_ID]',
    body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Walid' }),
});
// → { "greeting": "Hello, Walid! Welcome to Appwrite Functions. 👋" }
```

Or with `curl` against the function's domain:

```bash
curl -X POST https://[FUNCTION_ID].appwrite.global/ \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"name": "Walid"}'
```

**Note:** `req.bodyJson` is only populated when the request has a JSON content type — that's why setting the `Content-Type: application/json` header matters when calling the function over HTTP.

Here's a complete example. One important note first: **creating a bucket is an admin operation that requires an API key**, so it can't be done with the client-side Web SDK — it must be done with the server SDK (`node-appwrite`) or the Console/CLI. File upload and preview URLs, however, are done client-side with the Web SDK.

### Step 1: Create the bucket (server-side, Node.js)

```typescript
import { Client, Storage, ID, Permission, Role } from 'node-appwrite';

const adminClient = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const adminStorage = new Storage(adminClient);

const bucket = await adminStorage.createBucket({
    bucketId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'profile-images',
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.any()),       // anyone can view files
        Permission.create(Role.users()),   // only logged-in users can upload
        Permission.update(Role.users()),
        Permission.delete(Role.users()),
    ],
    fileSecurity: true,                    // also respect per-file permissions
    maximumFileSize: 5 * 1024 * 1024,      // 5 MB limit
    allowedFileExtensions: ['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'webp'],
});

console.log('Bucket created:', bucket.$id);
```

### Step 2: Upload a file and get a preview URL (client-side, Web SDK)

```typescript
import { Client, Storage, ID, Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const storage = new Storage(client);

async function uploadImage(currentUserId: string) {
    const input = document.getElementById('file-input') as HTMLInputElement;

    // Upload with per-file permissions (requires fileSecurity: true on the bucket)
    const file = await storage.createFile({
        bucketId: '[BUCKET_ID]',
        fileId: ID.unique(),
        file: input.files![0],
        permissions: [
            Permission.read(Role.any()),                 // publicly viewable
            Permission.update(Role.user(currentUserId)), // only owner can modify
            Permission.delete(Role.user(currentUserId)), // only owner can delete
        ],
    });

    console.log('File uploaded:', file.$id);

    // Get a preview URL (resized/transformed image) — returns a URL synchronously
    const previewUrl = storage.getFilePreview({
        bucketId: '[BUCKET_ID]',
        fileId: file.$id,
        width: 300,
        height: 300,
    });

    // Use it directly in an <img> tag
    const img = document.createElement('img');
    img.src = previewUrl.toString();
    document.body.appendChild(img);

    return previewUrl;
}
```

### Key points

- **Permissions model:** With `fileSecurity: true`, access is granted if *either* the bucket-level or file-level permissions allow it. If you don't need per-file rules, set permissions only at the bucket level and omit the `permissions` array in `createFile`.
- **`getFilePreview`** is synchronous — it just builds a URL. The image is transformed server-side (resize, crop, quality, format conversion, etc. are available as additional params). It only works on image files within size limits.
- **Don't forget permissions:** by default nothing is accessible — if you omit both bucket and file permissions, even the uploader can't read the file back.
- The preview endpoint requires `read` permission on the file, so `Permission.read(Role.any())` makes the preview URL work for unauthenticated visitors (e.g., embedding in public pages).

Here's how to subscribe to both table row changes and storage file events in a single realtime subscription using the Appwrite Web SDK, then branch your logic based on the event type:

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

// Subscribe to both channels at once
const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(), // all rows in a table
        Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file(),                        // all files in a bucket
    ],
    (response) => {
        const { events, payload, timestamp } = response;

        // --- Database row events ---
        if (events.some((e) => e.includes('.rows.'))) {
            if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.create'))) {
                console.log('New row created:', payload);
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.update'))) {
                console.log('Row updated:', payload);
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.delete'))) {
                console.log('Row deleted:', payload);
            }
            return;
        }

        // --- Storage file events ---
        if (events.some((e) => e.includes('.files.'))) {
            if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.create'))) {
                console.log('File uploaded:', payload); // payload is the file metadata
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.delete'))) {
                console.log('File deleted:', payload);
            }
        }
    }
);

// Later, when you no longer need updates:
await subscription.close();
```

**Key points:**

- **Single connection, multiple channels** — passing an array of channels to `realtime.subscribe()` multiplexes both streams over one WebSocket connection, which is more efficient than two separate subscriptions.
- **Event discrimination** — the `response.events` array contains event strings like:
  - `tablesdb.[DB_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows.[ROW_ID].create` (row created)
  - `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files.[FILE_ID].create` (file uploaded)

  Checking for `.rows.` vs `.files.` in the event string tells you which resource type fired, and the `.create`/`.update`/`.delete` suffix tells you the action.
- **Payload typing** — `payload` will be a row document (with `$id`, `$createdAt`, your columns, etc.) for database events, or a `Models.File` object (with `name`, `mimeType`, `sizeOriginal`, etc.) for storage events.
- **Permissions matter** — the subscriber only receives events for resources they have **read** permission on, so make sure your rows/files grant read access to the logged-in user (e.g., `Permission.read(Role.user(userId))`).
- **Cleanup** — call `subscription.close()` when the component unmounts or the listener is no longer needed to avoid leaked connections.

Here's a single `realtime.subscribe()` call that listens to both channels:

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

const subscription = await realtime.subscribe([
    Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(),
    Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file(),
], (response) => {
    console.log(response.events);   // event strings that fired
    console.log(response.channels); // which channel(s) matched
    console.log(response.payload);  // the affected row or file
});

// Later, when done:
await subscription.close();
```

**The exact channel strings** these builders produce are:

| Resource | Channel string |
|----------|---------------|
| All rows in a table | `tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows` |
| All files in a bucket | `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files` |

A few notes:

- You can also pass a specific ID to narrow the subscription: `.row('[ROW_ID]')` → `tablesdb.[DB_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows.[ROW_ID]`, or `.file('[FILE_ID]')` → `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files.[FILE_ID]`.
- Inside the callback, use `response.channels` or inspect `response.events` (e.g. `tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.create` vs. `buckets.*.files.*.create`) to distinguish row events from file events.
- Realtime subscriptions are **client-side only** — the user must have read permission on the rows/files to receive their events.

Here is how to implement email/password signup and login using the Appwrite Web SDK.

### 1. Installation

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

---

### 2. Implementation

```typescript
import { Client, Account, ID, AppwriteException } from 'appwrite';

// 1. Initialize the Appwrite Client
const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Or your custom endpoint / region
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                 // Replace with your Project ID

// 2. Initialize the Account service
const account = new Account(client);

/**
 * Sign up a new user and automatically log them in
 */
export async function signup(email: string, password: string, name?: string) {
    try {
        // Create account
        const user = await account.create({
            userId: ID.unique(),
            email: email,
            password: password,
            name: name // optional
        });

        console.log('Account created successfully:', user);

        // Create session (log in immediately after signup)
        const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
            email: email,
            password: password
        });

        console.log('Session started:', session);
        return { user, session };
    } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof AppwriteException) {
            console.error('Signup error:', error.message, error.code);
        }
        throw error;
    }
}

/**
 * Log in an existing user by creating an email/password session
 */
export async function login(email: string, password: string) {
    try {
        const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
            email: email,
            password: password
        });

        console.log('User logged in successfully:', session);
        return session;
    } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof AppwriteException) {
            console.error('Login error:', error.message, error.code);
        }
        throw error;
    }
}

/**
 * Get current authenticated user details
 */
export async function getCurrentUser() {
    try {
        return await account.get();
    } catch (error) {
        // Returns null if unauthenticated (401)
        return null;
    }
}

/**
 * Log out (delete the current session)
 */
export async function logout() {
    try {
        await account.deleteSession({
            sessionId: 'current'
        });
        console.log('User logged out successfully');
    } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof AppwriteException) {
            console.error('Logout error:', error.message);
        }
        throw error;
    }
}
```

---

### Key Highlights:
- **`ID.unique()`**: Generates a unique ID for the user upon creation.
- **`account.create()`**: Creates the user account with email, password, and an optional display name.
- **`account.createEmailPasswordSession()`**: Authenticates the credentials and creates a session cookie/token in the browser.
- **`account.get()`**: Fetches the currently authenticated user's profile.
- **`account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' })`**: Invalidates and logs out the active browser session.

In Appwrite, a **Session** represents an authenticated connection between a client (web browser, mobile device, or server) and an Appwrite project. It acts as the identity token authorizing API calls on behalf of a specific user.

---

### 1. How Appwrite Sessions Work

When a user logs in, Appwrite generates a session record and assigns a cryptographic secret.

#### Session Object Properties
Every session model in Appwrite includes rich metadata:
- **Identity:** `$id` (session ID), `userId` (associated user), `provider` (`email`, `anonymous`, `github`, `phone`, etc.).
- **Timestamps:** `$createdAt`, `expire` (ISO 8601 expiration date).
- **Client & Device Metadata:** `ip`, `osCode`, `osName`, `osVersion`, `clientType`, `clientName`, `clientVersion`, `deviceModel`, `deviceBrand`.
- **Geolocation:** `countryCode`, `countryName`.
- **State:** `current` (boolean indicating if the session belongs to the requesting client).

#### Platform Storage Mechanisms
- **Web Browsers (Client SDK):** Stored automatically in `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` cookies named `a_session_[PROJECT_ID]`.
- **Mobile & Desktop (Flutter, React Native, iOS, Android):** Stored securely in platform keychain/keystore and attached to requests via headers.
- **Server-Side Rendering (SSR / Node.js):** Stored in request cookies by your application server and injected into a per-request `Client` instance using `sessionClient.setSession(cookieSecret)`.

---

### 2. How Sessions are Created

Appwrite provides multiple authentication methods that result in an active session:

#### A. Email & Password
```typescript
import { Account, Client } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client().setEndpoint('https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1').setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');
const account = new Account(client);

const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
    email: 'user@example.com',
    password: 'securePassword123'
});
```

#### B. Anonymous (Guest) Session
Converts guest visitors into temporary users with full database/storage permissions:
```typescript
const session = await account.createAnonymousSession();
// Can later be upgraded to a full account with account.updateEmail(), etc.
```

#### C. OAuth 2.0
- **Browser:**
  ```typescript
  account.createOAuth2Session({
      provider: OAuthProvider.Github,
      success: 'https://example.com/dashboard',
      failure: 'https://example.com/login'
  });
  ```
- **Mobile / SSR (Token-based exchange):**
  1. Generate OAuth URL with `account.createOAuth2Token(...)`.
  2. Exchange the returned `userId` and `secret` via `account.createSession({ userId, secret })`.

#### D. Passwordless / OTP (Magic URL, Phone SMS, Email Token)
1. Send OTP / Token: `account.createEmailToken(...)` or `account.createPhoneToken(...)`.
2. Finalize session:
   ```typescript
   const session = await account.createSession({
       userId: '[USER_ID]',
       secret: '[OTP_OR_SECRET]'
   });
   ```

#### E. Server-Side / Admin Creation
Using the Server SDK (`node-appwrite`) with an API key, an admin can generate a session or custom token directly:
```typescript
import { Users, Client } from 'node-appwrite';

const users = new Users(adminClient);
const session = await users.createSession({
    userId: '[USER_ID]'
});
```

---

### 3. How Sessions are Managed

#### Viewing the Current Session & User
```typescript
// Fetch current user details
const user = await account.get();

// Fetch current session details
const currentSession = await account.getSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
```

#### Listing Active Sessions (Device Management UI)
Users can view all active logins across their phones, browsers, and desktops:
```typescript
// Client-side: list sessions for the current authenticated user
const response = await account.listSessions();

response.sessions.forEach(session => {
    console.log(`${session.clientName} on ${session.osName} (${session.ip})`);
    console.log(`Current device? ${session.current}`);
});
```

#### Admin Session Inspection
Administrators can inspect any user's active sessions:
```typescript
import { Users } from 'node-appwrite';

const users = new Users(adminClient);
const userSessions = await users.listSessions({ userId: '[USER_ID]' });
```

---

### 4. How Sessions are Deleted (Logout & Revocation)

#### A. Client-Side (User Actions)

- **Log out current device:**
  ```typescript
  await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
  ```

- **Log out a specific remote device (by Session ID):**
  ```typescript
  await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: '[SESSION_ID]' });
  ```

- **Log out all devices (Global Logout):**
  ```typescript
  await account.deleteSessions();
  ```

#### B. Admin / Server-Side Revocation
Admins can revoke sessions programmatically (e.g., when a user changes their password, is banned, or suspects a compromise):
```typescript
import { Users } from 'node-appwrite';

const users = new Users(adminClient);

// Invalidate a single session
await users.deleteSession({
    userId: '[USER_ID]',
    sessionId: '[SESSION_ID]'
});

// Invalidate ALL sessions for a user
await users.deleteSessions({
    userId: '[USER_ID]'
});
```

---

### 5. Session Limits and Expiration

| Setting | Default Value | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Max Concurrent Sessions** | **100** sessions per user | In self-hosted instances, configured via the `_APP_USERS_MAX_SESSIONS` environment variable. Once reached, creating a new session will reject or prune older sessions. |
| **Session Lifetime (Duration)** | **365 days** (1 year) | Configurable in the Appwrite Console (under *Auth Settings* > *Session Length*) or via `_APP_USERS_SESSION_LENGTH` in self-hosted deployments. |
| **Brute Force & Rate Limits** | Enabled | Appwrite applies built-in rate limiting on session creation endpoints (e.g., maximum attempts per minute per IP / account) to protect against credential stuffing. |

### Summary Flow
1. **Creation:** Initiated via credentials, OAuth, or OTP -> Returns a session object containing `$id`, `secret`, and `expire`.
2. **Verification:** Validated per request via `HttpOnly` cookie (Web) or `X-Appwrite-Session` header (Mobile/Server).
3. **Management:** Both users (`Account` API) and admins (`Users` API) can list and inspect device metadata.
4. **Termination:** Deleted individually (`'current'` / `[SESSION_ID]`) or bulk-revoked (`deleteSessions()`), or automatically invalidated once the expiration timestamp is reached.

Here is the code using the current Appwrite **`TablesDB`** API with object-based parameters to create a database, create the `tasks` table with the requested columns, and insert a row.

> **Note:** Creating databases and tables are administrative operations that require appropriate privileges (typically performed on the backend using an API key with `node-appwrite` or via an admin session). Inserting and reading rows can be done directly from the client.

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, ID } from 'appwrite';

// 1. Initialize the Appwrite Client and TablesDB
const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

async function setupAndInsertTask() {
    try {
        const databaseId = ID.unique();
        const tableId = ID.unique();

        // 2. Create the Database
        const database = await tablesDB.create({
            databaseId: databaseId,
            name: 'Task Management',
        });
        console.log('Database created:', database.$id);

        // 3. Create the "tasks" Table with columns
        const table = await tablesDB.createTable({
            databaseId: database.$id,
            tableId: tableId,
            name: 'tasks',
            columns: [
                {
                    key: 'title',
                    type: 'varchar',
                    size: 255,
                    required: true,
                },
                {
                    key: 'completed',
                    type: 'boolean',
                    required: true,
                    default: false,
                },
                {
                    key: 'dueDate',
                    type: 'datetime',
                    required: false,
                },
            ],
        });
        console.log('Table created:', table.$id);

        // 4. Insert a row into the "tasks" table
        const row = await tablesDB.createRow({
            databaseId: database.$id,
            tableId: table.$id,
            rowId: ID.unique(),
            data: {
                title: 'Review project specifications',
                completed: false,
                dueDate: new Date(Date.now() + 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000).toISOString(), // ISO 8601 timestamp
            },
        });
        console.log('Row inserted:', row);

        return row;
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error creating database, table, or row:', error);
    }
}

setupAndInsertTask();
```

### Key Notes:
- **`varchar` vs `text`:** `varchar` is stored inline and is fully indexable (up to 768 characters when indexed), making it ideal for short, searchable fields like titles.
- **`datetime` format:** Datetime column values must be passed as ISO 8601 formatted strings (e.g., `new Date().toISOString()`).
- **Object parameter syntax:** Modern Appwrite SDKs use the object parameter calling style (`{ databaseId, tableId, ... }`).

To query rows in Appwrite **TablesDB**, you pass an array of `Query` static methods to `tablesDB.listRows()`.

---

### Setup & Basic Usage

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite'; // Or 'node-appwrite' on the server

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);
```

---

### 1. Filtering

Queries are joined with a logical **AND** by default. Appwrite provides various filtering helpers:

```typescript
const filteredRows = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        // Exact match (pass an array for an "IN" condition)
        Query.equal('status', 'active'),
        Query.equal('role', ['admin', 'moderator']), // matches 'admin' OR 'moderator'

        // Inequality & Comparisons
        Query.notEqual('archived', true),
        Query.greaterThan('price', 50),
        Query.greaterThanEqual('rating', 4.5),
        Query.lessThan('stock', 10),
        Query.lessThanEqual('discount', 20),
        Query.between('age', 18, 65), // 18 <= age <= 65

        // Null checks
        Query.isNotNull('publishedAt'),
        // Query.isNull('deletedAt'),

        // String matching
        Query.startsWith('email', 'admin@'),
        Query.endsWith('domain', '.com'),
        Query.contains('tags', 'tech'), // Works on strings and array columns

        // Full-text search (requires a full-text index on the column)
        Query.search('title', 'tutorial guides')
    ]
});
```

#### Logical OR / Nested Logical Queries
To group conditions with **OR**, use `Query.or()`:

```typescript
const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.or([
            Query.equal('status', 'pending'),
            Query.greaterThan('priority', 5)
        ])
    ]
});
```

---

### 2. Sorting

Sort results in ascending (`orderAsc`) or descending (`orderDesc`) order by specifying column names. You can chain multiple sorting rules:

```typescript
const sortedRows = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        // Primary sort: newest first
        Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'),
        // Secondary sort: alphabetical by title
        Query.orderAsc('title')
    ]
});
```

---

### 3. Limiting

By default, Appwrite returns **25** rows per request. You can customize this up to a maximum of **100**:

```typescript
const topFive = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.orderDesc('score'),
        Query.limit(5) // Returns at most 5 rows
    ]
});
```

---

### 4. Pagination

There are two pagination methods: **Offset-based** and **Cursor-based**.

#### A. Offset-Based Pagination (Page Numbering)
Best for small datasets or when jumping directly to specific page numbers:

```typescript
const page = 3;
const limit = 20;
const offset = (page - 1) * limit;

const pageResults = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.limit(limit),
        Query.offset(offset) // Skips the first 40 rows
    ]
});

console.log(`Showing page ${page} of ${Math.ceil(pageResults.total / limit)}`);
```

#### B. Cursor-Based Pagination (Recommended for Large Datasets & Infinite Scroll)
Cursor pagination is much more performant on large datasets because it avoids scanning skipped rows. Pass the `$id` of the row after/before which you want to fetch:

```typescript
// 1. Initial page
const firstPage = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'),
        Query.limit(25)
    ]
});

// 2. Fetch the next page using the last row's ID
const lastRowId = firstPage.documents[firstPage.documents.length - 1]?.$id;

if (lastRowId) {
    const nextPage = await tablesDB.listRows({
        databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
        tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
        queries: [
            Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'),
            Query.limit(25),
            Query.cursorAfter(lastRowId) // Fetch rows after this ID
        ]
    });
}
```

> **Tip:** You can also use `Query.cursorBefore(rowId)` to paginate backwards.

---

### Comprehensive Example

Combining filtering, column selection, sorting, and pagination:

```typescript
const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: 'shop_db',
    tableId: 'products',
    queries: [
        // Select specific fields to reduce payload
        Query.select(['title', 'price', 'rating', 'stock']),

        // Filters
        Query.equal('category', 'electronics'),
        Query.greaterThanEqual('rating', 4.0),
        Query.greaterThan('stock', 0),

        // Sorting
        Query.orderAsc('price'),

        // Pagination
        Query.limit(20),
        Query.offset(0)
    ]
});

console.log(`Found ${results.total} total matching products:`);
results.documents.forEach((product) => {
    console.log(`${product.title} - $${product.price} (Rating: ${product.rating})`);
});
```

To filter rows where a numeric field is greater than `100`, limit the results to `20`, and sort them in descending order, you combine three methods from the `Query` class:

1. **`Query.greaterThan(attribute, value)`** — Filters rows where the field is strictly greater than the given value.
2. **`Query.orderDesc(attribute)`** — Orders the results by the field in descending order.
3. **`Query.limit(value)`** — Sets the maximum number of rows to return.

---

### Example (TypeScript / JavaScript)

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite'; 
// Or 'node-appwrite' for server-side code

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const response = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.greaterThan('price', 100), // 1. Filter: price > 100
        Query.orderDesc('price'),        // 2. Sort: descending by price
        Query.limit(20)                  // 3. Pagination: at most 20 results
    ]
});

console.log(response.documents);
```

---

### Key Details

- **Array of Queries:** All queries passed inside the `queries` array are applied together using an implicit `AND` condition.
- **Indexes:** Ensure you have created an index in the Appwrite Console for the field (e.g., `price`) to allow filtering and ordering on that attribute.
- **Results Structure:** `response.documents` contains the matching rows/documents up to the specified limit (20), and `response.total` provides the total number of matching rows in the table.

Here is an Appwrite Function written in Node.js that accepts a JSON body with a `name` field and returns a personalized greeting.

### `src/main.js`

```javascript
export default async ({ req, res, log, error }) => {
  // Check if the request method is POST
  if (req.method !== 'POST') {
    return res.json(
      { ok: false, error: 'Method not allowed. Please send a POST request.' },
      405
    );
  }

  // Appwrite automatically parses JSON payloads into req.bodyJson
  // We can also safely fallback if req.bodyText was passed
  let body = req.bodyJson;
  if (!body && req.bodyText) {
    try {
      body = JSON.parse(req.bodyText);
    } catch (e) {
      error(`Failed to parse JSON body: ${e.message}`);
      return res.json(
        { ok: false, error: 'Invalid JSON payload provided.' },
        400
      );
    }
  }

  const name = body?.name;

  if (!name || typeof name !== 'string' || name.trim() === '') {
    return res.json(
      { ok: false, error: 'Missing or invalid "name" field in request body.' },
      400
    );
  }

  log(`Generating greeting for: ${name}`);

  // Return JSON response with greeting
  return res.json({
    ok: true,
    message: `Hello, ${name.trim()}!`
  });
};
```

---

### `package.json`

Ensure your `package.json` specifies ES Module support (`"type": "module"`):

```json
{
  "name": "greeting-function",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "main": "src/main.js",
  "dependencies": {}
}
```

---

### How it Works:

- **Parameters (`{ req, res, log, error }`)**:
  - `req.bodyJson`: Automatically parsed JSON payload if `Content-Type: application/json` is sent.
  - `req.method`: The HTTP method (`GET`, `POST`, etc.).
  - `res.json(data, statusCode, headers)`: Helper to return a JSON response with status code (defaults to `200`).
  - `log(message)`: Logs informational output visible in the Appwrite Console execution logs.
  - `error(message)`: Logs errors visible in the Appwrite Console execution logs.

In Appwrite, **creating a bucket** is an administrative operation that requires an API key (server-side via `node-appwrite` or through the Appwrite Console / CLI) and should not be performed directly from a public browser client.

Once the bucket is created, the **Appwrite Web SDK (`appwrite`)** handles **uploading files** and **generating image preview URLs** on the client side.

---

### 1. Server-Side: Create a Bucket (with Permissions)

Use `node-appwrite` (or the Appwrite Console) to create the bucket with bucket-level permissions.

```bash
npm install node-appwrite
```

```typescript
import { Client, Storage, ID, Permission, Role } from 'node-appwrite';

const adminClient = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID!)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY!);

const storage = new Storage(adminClient);

async function createBucket() {
    const bucket = await storage.createBucket({
        bucketId: ID.unique(), // or specify an ID, e.g. 'images'
        name: 'User Images',
        permissions: [
            Permission.create(Role.users()), // Logged-in users can upload files
            Permission.read(Role.any()),     // Anyone can read files (public access)
            Permission.update(Role.users()), // Authenticated users can update
            Permission.delete(Role.users()), // Authenticated users can delete
        ],
        fileSecurity: true, // Enable to enforce per-file permissions
        enabled: true,
        maximumFileSize: 5 * 1024 * 1024, // 5 MB limit
        allowedFileExtensions: ['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'webp', 'gif'],
    });

    console.log('Bucket created:', bucket.$id);
    return bucket;
}

createBucket();
```

---

### 2. Client-Side (Web SDK): Upload a File & Get Image Preview

Install the Web SDK:

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

#### Complete Web SDK Code:

```typescript
import { Client, Storage, ID, Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';

// 1. Initialize the client
const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const storage = new Storage(client);

const BUCKET_ID = '[BUCKET_ID]';

/**
 * Upload an image file with custom permissions
 */
async function uploadImageFile(fileInput: File, currentUserId: string) {
    try {
        const file = await storage.createFile({
            bucketId: BUCKET_ID,
            fileId: ID.unique(),
            file: fileInput,
            permissions: [
                // Public read access for the preview/image
                Permission.read(Role.any()),
                // Only the uploader can update or delete this file
                Permission.update(Role.user(currentUserId)),
                Permission.delete(Role.user(currentUserId)),
            ],
        });

        console.log('Uploaded File ID:', file.$id);
        return file;
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error uploading file:', error);
        throw error;
    }
}

/**
 * Generate a preview URL for an image
 */
function getImagePreviewUrl(fileId: string) {
    // getFilePreview synchronously generates the URL with image transformations
    const previewUrl = storage.getFilePreview({
        bucketId: BUCKET_ID,
        fileId: fileId,
        width: 400,          // optional: resize width in px
        height: 400,         // optional: resize height in px
        quality: 80,         // optional: compression quality (0-100)
        output: 'webp',      // optional: output format ('jpg' | 'png' | 'webp' | 'gif')
    });

    return previewUrl.toString();
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------
// Usage Example in Browser (e.g., with HTML <input type="file">)
// -------------------------------------------------------------
async function handleImageUpload(event: Event) {
    const target = event.target as HTMLInputElement;
    const file = target.files?.[0];

    if (!file) return;

    const currentUserId = '[CURRENT_USER_ID]'; // e.g., from (await account.get()).$id

    // Upload
    const uploadedFile = await uploadImageFile(file, currentUserId);

    // Get image preview URL
    const previewUrl = getImagePreviewUrl(uploadedFile.$id);
    console.log('Preview URL:', previewUrl);

    // Display in <img> tag
    const imgElement = document.getElementById('preview-image') as HTMLImageElement;
    if (imgElement) {
        imgElement.src = previewUrl;
    }
}
```

---

### Permission Summary

| Role Target | Description | Example Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| `Role.any()` | Anyone, including unauthenticated guests | Public image preview / download |
| `Role.users()` | Any authenticated user | Allowing registered users to upload |
| `Role.user('[USER_ID]')` | Specific user only | Allowing file creator to edit/delete |
| `Role.team('[TEAM_ID]')` | Members of a specific team | Internal team file access |

**Appwrite Messaging** allows you to send push notifications, emails, and SMS messages across platforms.

---

### 1. Supported Push Notification Providers

For push notifications, Appwrite supports two primary providers:

- **FCM (Firebase Cloud Messaging):** Used for Android, Web push, and cross-platform apps (e.g., Flutter, React Native).
- **APNs (Apple Push Notification service):** Used natively for iOS, iPadOS, macOS, watchOS, and Safari.

---

### 2. End-to-End Setup Process

Setting up push notifications involves three main steps:

#### Step 1: Configure the Provider in Appwrite
1. Go to your **Appwrite Console** > **Messaging** > **Providers**.
2. Click **Add Provider** and select **Push**.
3. Choose your provider:
   - **For FCM:** Upload your Firebase Service Account private key JSON file.
   - **For APNs:** Provide your **Key ID**, **Team ID**, **Bundle ID**, and upload the `.p8` authentication token file.

---

#### Step 2: Register Device Tokens as Targets (Client-Side)

When a user logs into your app, capture their device push token (from Firebase Messaging or Apple APNs) and register it as a **Target** in Appwrite:

```typescript
import { Client, Account, ID } from 'appwrite'; // or react-native-appwrite / Flutter SDK

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const account = new Account(client);

// Register device push token to the currently authenticated user
await account.createPushTarget({
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    identifier: '[DEVICE_PUSH_TOKEN]', // FCM registration token or APNs device token
    providerId: '[PROVIDER_ID]',       // Optional if you only have one push provider
});
```

---

#### Step 3: Create a Topic and Subscribe Targets

Topics allow you to group multiple targets (or users) to broadcast notifications.

##### Server-Side Topic Creation and Subscription:
```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

// 1. Create a Topic
const topic = await messaging.createTopic({
    topicId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'Announcements',
});

// 2. Subscribe a user target to the topic
await messaging.createSubscriber({
    topicId: topic.$id,
    subscriberId: ID.unique(),
    targetId: '[TARGET_ID]', // ID from account.createPushTarget()
});
```

---

### 3. How to Send a Push Notification to a Topic

#### Option A: Using the Server SDK (`node-appwrite`)

Use the `createPush` method on the `Messaging` service. Specify the topic ID in the `topics` array:

```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

async function sendTopicNotification() {
    const response = await messaging.createPush({
        messageId: ID.unique(),
        title: 'New Announcement! 🚀',
        body: 'Check out the new features available in version 2.0.',
        topics: ['[TOPIC_ID]'],          // Target one or more topic IDs
        data: {                          // Optional: Custom key-value payload for deep linking
            route: '/announcements/123',
            priority: 'high'
        },
        action: 'https://example.com',   // Optional: URL / deep link to open on click
        // draft: false,                 // Default false; set true to save as draft
        // scheduledAt: '2025-01-01T12:00:00.000Z' // Optional: Schedule for later (ISO 8601)
    });

    console.log('Push notification dispatched:', response.$id);
}

sendTopicNotification();
```

#### Option B: Using the Appwrite Console (No-code)

1. Open your project in the **Appwrite Console**.
2. Navigate to **Messaging** > **Messages** > **Create Message**.
3. Select **Push notification**.
4. Under **Recipients**, select **Topics** and choose your desired topic.
5. Enter the **Title**, **Body**, and optional custom data/actions.
6. Click **Send immediately** (or schedule for a future date).

Here is complete, type-safe code using the Appwrite Web SDK to subscribe to both table row changes and file uploads in a single realtime subscription, handling each event separately based on its event type.

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime, Channel, Models } from 'appwrite';

// 1. Initialize the client
const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

// Replace with your actual IDs
const DATABASE_ID = '<DATABASE_ID>';
const TABLE_ID = '<TABLE_ID>';
const BUCKET_ID = '<BUCKET_ID>';

// Optional: define an interface for your table row data
interface ArticleRow extends Models.Document {
    title: string;
    content: string;
}

// 2. Subscribe to both table row and storage bucket channels
const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb(DATABASE_ID).table(TABLE_ID).row(), // All rows in table
        Channel.bucket(BUCKET_ID).file(),                    // All files in bucket
    ],
    (response) => {
        const events = response.events;

        // --- Handle Database / Table Row Events ---
        const isRowEvent = events.some((event) => event.includes('.rows.'));

        if (isRowEvent) {
            const row = response.payload as ArticleRow;

            if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.create'))) {
                console.log('Row created:', row.$id, row);
                // Handle new row (e.g., prepend to UI list)
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.update'))) {
                console.log('Row updated:', row.$id, row);
                // Handle row update (e.g., update item in UI state)
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.delete'))) {
                console.log('Row deleted:', row.$id);
                // Handle row deletion (e.g., remove item from UI state)
            }
            return;
        }

        // --- Handle Storage / File Events ---
        const isFileEvent = events.some((event) => event.includes('.files.'));

        if (isFileEvent) {
            const file = response.payload as Models.File;

            if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.create'))) {
                console.log('File uploaded:', file.$id, file.name, `${file.sizeOriginal} bytes`);
                // Handle new file upload (e.g., refresh gallery or show upload notice)
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.delete'))) {
                console.log('File deleted:', file.$id);
                // Handle file deletion
            }
            return;
        }
    }
);

// 3. Unsubscribe when no longer needed (e.g., component unmount)
// await subscription.close();
```

### Key Concepts

- **Multi-channel subscription**: Passing an array of channels (`[Channel.tablesdb(...), Channel.bucket(...)]`) to `realtime.subscribe()` multiplexes events over a single WebSocket connection.
- **Event discrimination**: `response.events` is an array of strings (e.g., `tablesdb.[DB_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows.[ROW_ID].create` or `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files.[FILE_ID].create`). Inspecting whether an event contains `.rows.` or `.files.` allows you to route payloads to appropriate handlers.
- **Payload types**: Cast `response.payload` to `Models.Document` (or a custom interface extending it) for row changes, and `Models.File` for storage changes.
- **Cleanup**: Call `await subscription.close()` when finished to close the subscription and prevent memory leaks.

### Exact Channel Strings

The exact channel strings are:

- **TablesDB Table Rows:** `tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows`
- **Storage Bucket Files:** `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files`

---

### `realtime.subscribe()` Call

You can pass multiple channels as an array directly to `realtime.subscribe()` using raw channel strings or using the `Channel` helper class:

#### Option 1: Using Raw Channel Strings

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        'tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows',
        'buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files'
    ],
    (response) => {
        console.log('Events:', response.events);
        console.log('Payload:', response.payload);
    }
);

// To unsubscribe:
// await subscription.close();
```

#### Option 2: Using the `Channel` Helper

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(),
        Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file()
    ],
    (response) => {
        console.log('Events:', response.events);
        console.log('Payload:', response.payload);
    }
);
```

In Appwrite there are 2 SDK variants - they use the same API but have different privileges:

### 1. Client-side SDK - `appwrite` / `react-native-appwrite`

For **untrusted** environments: browser, React Native, Flutter, etc. Acts **as the logged-in user** and respects permissions.

```typescript
// Web
import { Client, Account, TablesDB, Storage } from 'appwrite';
// React Native
import { Client, Account, TablesDB, Storage } from 'react-native-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
  .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');
// NO API key - uses session cookie/token
```

**Capabilities:**
* `Account` - `create()`, `createEmailPasswordSession()`, `createOAuth2Session()` / `createOAuth2Token()` (React Native), `get()`, `deleteSession()`
* `TablesDB` / `Storage` - `createRow()`, `listRows()`, `createFile()` etc. **only if permissions allow**
* `Realtime` - `realtime.subscribe(Channel.tablesdb(...).row(), callback)` - **client-only**

**Cannot do:** `Users`, `Teams` admin, create Database/Table, bypass permissions. Never has API key.

### 2. Server-side SDK - `node-appwrite`

For **trusted** environments: Node.js/Deno server, Appwrite Function, SSR backend (Next.js/SvelteKit/Nuxt). Uses API Key and can bypass permissions.

```typescript
import { Client, Users, TablesDB } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
  .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID)
  .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY); // keep secret, never expose to client
```

**Capabilities:**
* Everything client can do **plus privileged services**: `Users` (`users.create()`, `users.list()`, `users.delete()`), `Functions`, creating Databases/Tables, `InputFile.fromPath()/fromBuffer()/fromStream()`
* Can act as admin **or** on behalf of a user:

```typescript
// Admin client - reusable singleton, bypasses permissions
const adminClient = new Client().setEndpoint(...).setProject(...).setKey(...);

// Session client - per-request, respects user permissions
const sessionClient = new Client().setEndpoint(...).setProject(...).setSession(sessionSecret);
sessionClient.setForwardedUserAgent(req.headers['user-agent']);
```

### When to use which?

| Use Client-side when | Use Server-side when |
|---|---|
| Building UI that logs in users, reads/writes data as *that user* | You need admin operations: manage users, create databases/tables, delete any file |
| You need Realtime subscriptions | Running in Appwrite Functions, webhooks, cron jobs, background workers |
| Direct file upload from `<input type="file">` | Uploading from filesystem `InputFile.fromPath()` |
| Code runs in browser/mobile | Code runs on server / SSR - need to set `a_session_<PROJECT_ID>` cookie with `httpOnly, secure, sameSite: strict` |

**Rule of thumb:** If it needs `APPWRITE_API_KEY` or should bypass permissions -> server-side. If it runs on the user's device and should be limited by permissions/roles -> client-side. Never expose the API key to the client.

Appwrite Web SDK - Email/Password Auth:

### 1. Installation & Client Setup

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

```typescript
import { Client, Account, ID } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // e.g. https://fra.cloud.appwrite.io/v1
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const account = new Account(client);
```

### 2. Create Account (Signup)

```typescript
async function signUp(email: string, password: string, name: string) {
    // Creates the user account
    await account.create({
        userId: ID.unique(),
        email: email,
        password: password,
        name: name
    });

    // Automatically log in after signup by creating a session
    const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
        email: email,
        password: password
    });

    console.log('Signed up and logged in:', session);
    return session;
}

// Usage
await signUp('user@example.com', 'password123', 'User Name');
```

### 3. Create Session (Login)

```typescript
async function login(email: string, password: string) {
    const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
        email: email,
        password: password
    });

    console.log('Logged in:', session);
    return session;
}

// Usage
await login('user@example.com', 'password123');
```

### 4. Helpers - Get User & Logout

```typescript
// Get currently logged in user
const user = await account.get();
console.log(user);

// Logout (delete current session)
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
```

In Appwrite, a `Session` = authenticated state for a `User`. Created via `Account` service, stored server-side, client holds only `secret`.

### 1. Creation

`secret` is returned **only on creation** - store it as cookie `a_session_<PROJECT_ID>` or via `client.setSession(secret)`. All creation methods return `Models.Session` (`$id`, `userId`, `expire`, `provider`, `ip`, `osName`, `clientName`, `countryName`):

```typescript
import { Client, Account, ID, OAuthProvider } from 'appwrite'
const client = new Client().setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1').setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
const account = new Account(client)

// Email/Password
const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
  email: 'user@example.com',
  password: 'password123'
})

// Anonymous -> can later be converted to permanent account
await account.createAnonymousSession()

// Phone (SMS)
await account.createPhoneSession({ userId: ID.unique(), phone: '+14155552671' })
await account.updatePhoneSession({ userId: '[USER_ID]', secret: '123456' })

// Magic URL
await account.createMagicURLToken({ userId: ID.unique(), email: 'user@example.com' })
await account.createSession({ userId: '[USER_ID]', secret: '[SECRET_FROM_URL]' })

// OAuth2 - Web (redirect)
account.createOAuth2Session({
  provider: OAuthProvider.Github,
  success: 'https://example.com/success',
  failure: 'https://example.com/fail'
})

// OAuth2 - SSR / React Native (token + createSession)
const loginUrl = await account.createOAuth2Token({
  provider: OAuthProvider.Google,
  success: 'https://example.com/success',
  failure: 'https://example.com/fail'
})
// after redirect back with ?userId=xxx&secret=yyy
await account.createSession({ userId: 'xxx', secret: 'yyy' })
```

For SSR (`node-appwrite`) you must set the cookie yourself:

```typescript
res.cookie(`a_session_${PROJECT_ID}`, session.secret, {
  httpOnly: true, secure: true, sameSite: 'strict',
  expires: new Date(session.expire), path: '/'
})
// per-request client
client.setSession(req.cookies[`a_session_${PROJECT_ID}`])
client.setForwardedUserAgent(req.headers['user-agent'])
```

Session length default `365 days`, configurable in Console > Auth > Security > Session length.

### 2. Management

```typescript
// Get current authenticated user (401 if no/invalid session)
const user = await account.get()

// List all active sessions for current user
const sessions = await account.listSessions()

// Get one session
const current = await account.getSession({ sessionId: 'current' }) // or specific $id

// Extend expiry (renews `expire`)
await account.updateSession({ sessionId: 'current' })
```

Server-side with API Key (`node-appwrite`):

```typescript
import { Users } from 'node-appwrite'
const users = new Users(adminClient)
await users.listSessions({ userId: '[USER_ID]' })
await users.getSession({ userId: '[USER_ID]', sessionId: '[SESSION_ID]' })
```

Client SDK automatically sends `X-Fallback-Cookies` header for browsers blocking 3rd-party cookies.

### 3. Deletion / Logout

```typescript
// Logout current device
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' })

// Logout specific device
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: '[SESSION_ID]' })

// Logout all devices
await account.deleteSessions()
```

Server-side (admin):

```typescript
await users.deleteSession({ userId: '[USER_ID]', sessionId: '[SESSION_ID]' })
await users.deleteSessions({ userId: '[USER_ID]' }) // force logout everywhere
```

Deleting the user also deletes all sessions.

### 4. Session Limits

* **Limit: 10 active sessions per user** by default (max 100 on Cloud Pro/Scale, configurable self-hosted via `_APP_MAX_SESSIONS`).
* When limit is reached, **creating a new session automatically deletes the oldest session** - no error is thrown. Use `listSessions()` + `deleteSession()` if you want to enforce stricter logic (e.g. single-device login).
* Anonymous sessions count toward the limit.
* Handle `401 Unauthorized` -> redirect to login and clear `a_session_*` cookie.

> Best practice: Always use `sessionId: 'current'` for logout on current device, and `deleteSessions()` for "Logout everywhere" / password change flows.

In Appwrite TypeScript SDK (`Account`), both log out user(s) but at different scope:

### 1. `account.deleteSession({ sessionId })` - Delete ONE session

Deletes a single session by ID.

```typescript
import { Client, Account } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
  .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const account = new Account(client);

// 1. Logout current device (most common - for a Logout button)
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });

// 2. Revoke a specific device/session (from a device management page)
const sessions = await account.listSessions();
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: sessions.sessions[0].$id });
```

**When to use:**
* Standard `Logout` button -> use `sessionId: 'current'` to only log out this browser/device, keeping other devices logged in.
* `My Devices / Active Sessions` page -> let user revoke a single suspicious session without logging them out everywhere.
* `current` is a keyword for the session tied to the current client cookie/header. Any other value must be a valid `$id` from `account.listSessions()`.

### 2. `account.deleteSessions()` - Delete ALL sessions

Deletes **every** session for the currently authenticated user. Takes no parameters.

```typescript
await account.deleteSessions();
// User is now logged out on ALL browsers/devices
```

**When to use:**
* `Log out everywhere` / `Sign out of all devices` feature.
* Security action after password change, email change, or suspected account compromise.
* Before account deletion.

> After either call on SSR (Next.js/SvelteKit), also clear the cookie `a_session_<PROJECT_ID>` on the client.

| | `deleteSession({sessionId})` | `deleteSessions()` |
|---|---|---|
| **Scope** | 1 session | All sessions for current user |
| **Param** | `sessionId: string` (`'current'` or `$id`) | none |
| **Keeps other devices logged in?** | Yes | No |

Current `TablesDB` API (`appwrite` Web SDK) - uses object-params and `columns` inline on `createTable`:

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, ID } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // e.g. https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

async function createTasksExample() {
  // 1. Create database - requires API key / privileged session (server-side)
  const db = await tablesDB.create({
    databaseId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'My Database'
  });

  // 2. Create "tasks" table with columns
  const table = await tablesDB.createTable({
    databaseId: db.$id,
    tableId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'tasks',
    columns: [
      { key: 'title', type: 'varchar', size: 255, required: true },
      { key: 'completed', type: 'boolean', required: false, default: false },
      { key: 'dueDate', type: 'datetime', required: false }
    ]
  });

  // 3. Insert a row
  const row = await tablesDB.createRow({
    databaseId: db.$id,
    tableId: table.$id,
    rowId: ID.unique(),
    data: {
      title: 'Buy groceries',
      completed: false,
      dueDate: new Date('2026-12-31T23:59:00.000Z').toISOString() // datetime = ISO 8601 string
    }
  });

  console.log(row);
  return row;
}

createTasksExample();
```

> **Note:** `tablesDB.create()` and `tablesDB.createTable()` require server privileges (API key). For production, run steps 1-2 with `node-appwrite` on the server. `tablesDB.createRow()` works client-side for authenticated users. `varchar` requires `size` (max 16383), `boolean` and `datetime` do not.

In Appwrite `TablesDB`, you query rows with `tablesDB.listRows()` + the `Query` class. All queries are passed as an array to `queries:` - they are `AND` by default.

### 1. Setup

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite';
// Server: import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
  .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);
```

### 2. Filtering

```typescript
// Equality - single value or array (IN query)
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [Query.equal('status', 'active')]
});
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [Query.equal('status', ['active', 'draft'])] // status IN ['active','draft']
});

// Comparison
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.notEqual('status', 'archived'),
    Query.lessThan('price', 100),
    Query.lessThanEqual('price', 100),
    Query.greaterThan('price', 10),
    Query.greaterThanEqual('price', 10),
    Query.between('price', 10, 100) // 10 <= price <= 100
  ]
});

// Null checks
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [Query.isNull('deletedAt'), Query.isNotNull('publishedAt')]
});

// String search
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.startsWith('name', 'Alex'),
    Query.endsWith('email', '@example.com'),
    Query.contains('tags', 'featured'), // string/array contains value
    Query.search('title', 'appwrite tutorial') // full-text search - requires full-text index
  ]
});

// Logical OR / AND
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.or([
      Query.equal('status', 'active'),
      Query.equal('status', 'draft')
    ])
  ]
});

await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.and([
      Query.greaterThan('age', 18),
      Query.lessThan('age', 65)
    ])
  ]
});
// Note: Multiple queries are AND by default, so this is equivalent:
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [Query.greaterThan('age', 18), Query.lessThan('age', 65)]
});
```

### 3. Sorting

```typescript
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.orderAsc('createdAt'),   // oldest first
    Query.orderDesc('price')       // highest price first
  ]
});

// Combine filtering + sorting
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.equal('status', 'active'),
    Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'), // you can sort by system fields like $createdAt, $updatedAt
    Query.orderAsc('title')
  ]
});
```

### 4. Limiting & Field Selection

```typescript
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.limit(10), // max rows returned - default 25, max 100
    Query.select(['title', 'price', 'status']) // return only these fields
  ]
});
```

### 5. Pagination

Two methods: `offset` (simple, but slow for large datasets) and `cursor` (preferred).

**Offset pagination:**
```typescript
// Page 1
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.offset(0)]
});

// Page 2
await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.offset(25)]
});
```

**Cursor pagination (recommended for large datasets):**
```typescript
// First page
const firstPage = await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.orderAsc('$createdAt')]
});

// Next page - pass last document's $id
const nextPage = await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.limit(25),
    Query.orderAsc('$createdAt'),
    Query.cursorAfter(firstPage.documents[firstPage.documents.length - 1].$id)
  ]
});

// Previous page
const prevPage = await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.limit(25),
    Query.orderAsc('$createdAt'),
    Query.cursorBefore(nextPage.documents[0].$id)
  ]
});
```

### 6. Combined Example

```typescript
const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.equal('status', 'active'),
    Query.greaterThan('price', 20),
    Query.contains('tags', 'sale'),
    Query.orderDesc('price'),
    Query.limit(10),
    Query.offset(20),
    Query.select(['title', 'price', 'status'])
  ]
});

console.log(results.total); // total matching rows
console.log(results.documents); // array of rows with $id, $createdAt, $updatedAt, etc.
```
> **Tip:** `Query.search()` requires a full-text index on that field. `Query.limit()` max is 100. Use `cursorAfter`/`cursorBefore` instead of `offset` for performance on large tables.

To filter by `> 100`, limit to `20`, and sort descending use 3 `Query` helpers in `TablesDB.listRows()`:

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite';
// Server-side: import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'node-appwrite'
// React Native: import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'react-native-appwrite'

const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
  .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
  databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
  tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
  queries: [
    Query.greaterThan('price', 100), // filter: price > 100
    Query.orderDesc('price'),        // sort: price descending
    Query.limit(20)                  // pagination: at most 20 rows
  ]
});
```

* `Query.greaterThan('field', 100)` -> `field > 100` . Use `Query.greaterThanEqual('field', 100)` for `>= 100`.
* `Query.orderDesc('field')` -> sort descending by that field. Use `Query.orderAsc('field')` for ascending.
* `Query.limit(20)` -> max rows returned (default `25`, max `100`).

> Replace `'price'` with your numeric field name. All queries in the array are `AND`ed together by default.

Appwrite Function in Node.js - personalized greeting:

**`src/main.js` (Node.js 18+ ESM runtime):**

```javascript
export default async ({ req, res, log, error }) => {
  try {
    // Appwrite parses JSON automatically to req.bodyJson
    // Fallback to manual parse for raw body
    const payload = req.bodyJson ?? JSON.parse(req.body || '{}');

    const name = payload.name?.trim();

    if (!name) {
      return res.json({
        success: false,
        message: 'Missing "name" field. Send JSON: { "name": "YourName" }'
      }, 400);
    }

    log(`Greeting request for: ${name}`);

    return res.json({
      success: true,
      message: `Hello, ${name}!`
    });

  } catch (err) {
    error(err.message);
    return res.json({
      success: false,
      message: 'Invalid JSON body'
    }, 400);
  }
};
```

**CommonJS alternative if `package.json` is not `"type": "module"`:**

```javascript
module.exports = async ({ req, res, log, error }) => {
  const payload = req.bodyJson ?? JSON.parse(req.body || '{}');
  const name = payload.name;
  if (!name) {
    return res.json({ message: 'Missing "name" field' }, 400);
  }
  return res.json({ message: `Hello, ${name}!` });
}
```

### Key Details

1.  **Signature:** `export default async ({ req, res, log, error }) => {}` is the correct signature for Appwrite Functions `1.4+`. Don't use `(req, res)` or `context`.
2.  **Request Body:** Use `req.bodyJson` for parsed JSON, or `JSON.parse(req.body)` if raw. `req.body` is always a string.
3.  **Response Format:** Always `return res.json(object, statusCode)` / `res.text(string, statusCode)` / `res.empty()`. Don't use `res.send()` or return a plain object.

**Test:**

```bash
curl -X POST https://<FUNCTION_DOMAIN> \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"name": "Alex"}'

# Response: {"success":true,"message":"Hello, Alex!"}
```

Using `appwrite` Web SDK - bucket creation, file upload with permissions, and image preview:

> **Note:** Bucket creation is an admin operation. In production create buckets from the Console or with `node-appwrite` + API Key. The Web SDK can create buckets only if the authenticated user has permission.

```typescript
import { Client, Storage, ID, Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // e.g. https://fra.cloud.appwrite.io/v1
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const storage = new Storage(client);

// 1. Create bucket with permissions
async function createBucket() {
  const bucket = await storage.createBucket({
    bucketId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'Images',
    permissions: [
      Permission.create(Role.users()), // any logged-in user can upload
      Permission.read(Role.any()),     // anyone can read/list files
      Permission.update(Role.users()),
      Permission.delete(Role.users())
    ],
    fileSecurity: true, // enable per-file permissions (required to set permissions on createFile)
    maximumFileSize: 5 * 1024 * 1024, // 5MB
    allowedFileExtensions: ['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'webp'],
    encryption: true,
    antivirus: false,
    enabled: true
  });
  console.log('Bucket created:', bucket.$id);
  return bucket;
}

// 2. Upload file with per-file permissions
async function uploadImage(bucketId: string, userId: string) {
  const fileInput = document.getElementById('file-input') as HTMLInputElement;
  if (!fileInput.files?.[0]) throw new Error('No file selected');

  const file = await storage.createFile({
    bucketId: bucketId,
    fileId: ID.unique(),
    file: fileInput.files[0],
    permissions: [
      Permission.read(Role.any()), // public preview - remove for private files
      Permission.update(Role.user(userId)),
      Permission.delete(Role.user(userId))
    ]
  });
  console.log('File uploaded:', file.$id);
  return file;
}

// 3. Get preview URL for image (returns string URL, not a Promise)
function getPreviewUrl(bucketId: string, fileId: string) {
  const previewUrl = storage.getFilePreview({
    bucketId: bucketId,
    fileId: fileId,
    width: 400,  // resize for preview
    height: 400
  });

  // Use directly in <img>
  (document.getElementById('preview') as HTMLImageElement).src = previewUrl;

  return previewUrl;
}

// Usage
async function run(userId: string) {
  const bucket = await createBucket();
  const file = await uploadImage(bucket.$id, userId);
  const url = getPreviewUrl(bucket.$id, file.$id);
  console.log(url);
}
```

```html
<input type="file" id="file-input" accept="image/*" />
<img id="preview" alt="preview" />
```

### Permission Configuration

```typescript
import { Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';

// Bucket-level (who can create/read/update/delete files in bucket)
Permission.create(Role.users())      // any authenticated user can upload
Permission.read(Role.any())          // public read (needed for public preview)
Permission.read(Role.users())        // only logged-in users can read
Permission.read(Role.team('teamId')) // only team members

// File-level (requires fileSecurity: true on bucket)
Permission.read(Role.any())                 // public preview
Permission.read(Role.user('[USER_ID]'))     // only owner can read
Permission.update(Role.user('[USER_ID]'))
Permission.delete(Role.user('[USER_ID]'))
```

> **Critical:**
> - If `fileSecurity: false`, file-level `permissions` are ignored and bucket permissions apply.
> - Never use `Permission.write(Role.any())` or `Permission.update(Role.any())` - allows guests to modify/delete.
> - `getFilePreview()` is synchronous and returns a URL string. Use `storage.getFileView()` for original file without transformation, or `storage.getFileDownload()` for download.

In Appwrite `Messaging` push is **server-side only** (`node-appwrite` + API Key). Client SDK is only used to register the device token.

### 1. Supported Push Providers

Appwrite Messaging supports **2** push providers:

| Provider | Platform | Credentials Required |
|---|---|---|
| **FCM** - Firebase Cloud Messaging | Android, Web Push, iOS (via FCM) | Firebase Service Account JSON (`Project Settings > Service Accounts > Generate new private key`) |
| **APNS** - Apple Push Notification Service | iOS, macOS, Safari | `Team ID`, `Key ID`, `Bundle ID`, `p8` private key, `Sandbox/Production` |

> Email (SMTP, Mailgun, SendGrid) and SMS (Twilio, Vonage, Telesign, TextMagic, MSG91) are separate provider types - not for push.

Create provider in Console: `Messaging > Providers > Create Provider > Push > FCM / APNS` or via server SDK `messaging.createFcmProvider()` / `messaging.createApnsProvider()`.

### 2. Setup Flow

```text
1. Create Provider (FCM/APNS) -> 2. Create Topic -> 3. Register Device as Push Target -> 4. Subscribe Target to Topic -> 5. Send Push to Topic
```

**a) Create Topic (server):**
```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
  .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID)
  .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

const topic = await messaging.createTopic({
  topicId: ID.unique(),
  name: 'news',
  subscribe: ['any'] // who can subscribe, e.g. Role.users()
});
```

**b) Register Device Token (client - Web / React Native):**
Get token from Firebase (`getToken(messaging)`) or APNS (`getDeviceToken()`), then register:

```typescript
import { Client, Account, ID } from 'appwrite';
// import { Client, Account, ID } from 'react-native-appwrite';

const client = new Client().setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1').setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');
const account = new Account(client);

// identifier = FCM registration token or APNS device token
await account.createPushTarget({
  targetId: ID.unique(),
  identifier: 'DEVICE_TOKEN_FROM_FCM_OR_APNS',
  providerId: '[FCM_OR_APNS_PROVIDER_ID]' // from step 1
});
```

**c) Subscribe Target to Topic (server):**
```typescript
await messaging.createSubscriber({
  topicId: topic.$id,
  subscriberId: ID.unique(),
  targetId: '[TARGET_ID]' // $id of the push target created above
});
```

### 3. Send Push to Topic (server)

Requires API Key with `messaging` scope.

```typescript
import { ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const message = await messaging.createPush({
  messageId: ID.unique(),
  title: 'Breaking News',
  body: 'New article published!',
  topics: [topic.$id], // <- send to topic
  // optional: users: [], targets: [] // alternative to topics
  data: { url: '/articles/123' }, // custom payload
  action: 'Open Article',
  image: 'https://example.com/image.png',
  icon: 'https://example.com/icon.png',
  sound: 'default',
  color: '#ff0000',
  tag: 'news',
  badge: 1,
  draft: false, // true = don't send, save as draft
  // scheduledAt: '2026-08-25T10:00:00.000Z' // schedule for later
});

console.log(message.$id);
```

You can target `topics`, `users`, and `targets` together - message is sent to the union. Leave `draft: false` (default) to send immediately. Check delivery in `Console > Messaging > Messages` or via `messaging.listMessageLogs()`.

> Test from Console: `Messaging > Messages > Create Message > Push` without code.

In Appwrite `Messaging` (server-side only - `node-appwrite`) all messages are sent via `providers` (FCM/APNS for Push, SMTP/SendGrid/Mailgun for Email, Twilio/Vonage for SMS) and targeted with 3 mutually combinable parameters on `createEmail`/`createPush`/`createSms` - at least one is required (OR logic):

```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
  .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID)
  .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const messaging = new Messaging(client);
```

### 1. `users: string[]` - Specific User(s)
Sends to **all targets** belonging to the user(s). A user can have multiple targets (devices/emails/phones).

```typescript
// Push to 2 users (all their push targets)
await messaging.createPush({
  messageId: ID.unique(),
  title: "New message",
  body: "You have a new notification",
  users: ['userId1', 'userId2']
});

// Email to users (sends to their email targets)
await messaging.createEmail({
  messageId: ID.unique(),
  subject: "Welcome",
  content: "Hello {{name}}",
  users: ['userId1']
});

// SMS to users (sends to their phone targets)
await messaging.createSms({
  messageId: ID.unique(),
  content: "Your code is 123456",
  users: ['userId1']
});
```

### 2. `topics: string[]` - Pub/Sub Topics
Sends to all subscribers of a topic. Best for broadcast/segments (e.g. `news`, `premium-users`).

Create topic and subscribe targets first:

```typescript
// 1. Create topic
const topic = await messaging.createTopic({
  topicId: ID.unique(),
  name: 'announcements'
});

// 2. Subscribe a target to topic (targetId from Users/Account targets)
await messaging.createSubscriber({
  topicId: topic.$id,
  subscriberId: ID.unique(),
  targetId: '[TARGET_ID]' // e.g. push target ID from account.createPushTarget()
});

// 3. Send to topic
await messaging.createPush({
  messageId: ID.unique(),
  title: "Announcement",
  body: "New feature released!",
  topics: [topic.$id]
});
```
Manage with `messaging.listTopics()`, `listSubscribers()`, `deleteSubscriber()`.

### 3. `targets: string[]` - Individual Target (Most Granular)
Sends to a single delivery channel ID. A target is one device token / email / phone number.

Targets are created by the user or server:

```typescript
// Client: user registers push target
// import { Account } from 'appwrite'
// await account.createPushTarget({ targetId: ID.unique(), identifier: 'fcm-token...' })

// Server: list targets
import { Users } from 'node-appwrite';
const users = new Users(client);
const { targets } = await users.listTargets({ userId: '[USER_ID]' });

// Send to exact target only
await messaging.createPush({
  messageId: ID.unique(),
  title: "Personal alert",
  body: "Only this device will receive it",
  targets: ['[TARGET_ID]']
});
```

### Combined Targeting

You can combine arrays - message is delivered to union of all:

```typescript
await messaging.createPush({
  messageId: ID.unique(),
  title: "Update",
  body: "Hello!",
  users: ['userA'],
  topics: ['announcements'],
  targets: ['specificTargetId']
});
```

> **Requires:** Configured provider in Console > Messaging > Providers, and `draft`/`scheduledAt`/`deliveredAt` handling is automatic. All `Messaging` methods require API key with `messages.write` scope - never from client SDK.

In `node-appwrite` push to a `Topic` requires `Messaging` + a configured Push Provider. You cannot send until provider is configured.

### 1. What Must Be Configured First (Server - one time)

**a) Push Provider in Console `Messaging > Providers`:**
* **FCM (Android/Web/Flutter):** Firebase Service Account JSON + Project ID
* **APNS (iOS):** `.p8` Key + Key ID + Team ID + Bundle ID

Without this, `createPush` will fail - no delivery channel.

**b) Create Topic:**
Console `Messaging > Topics > Create Topic` or via SDK (server with API Key `messaging` scope):

```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
  .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID)
  .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

await messaging.createTopic({
  topicId: 'announcements',
  name: 'Announcements',
  subscribe: ['any'] // who can subscribe, e.g. Role.users() / Role.any()
});
```

**c) Users must have a Push Target and be Subscribed:**
Client-side (after login + OS permission granted, you get `deviceToken` from FCM/APNS):

```typescript
import { Client, Account, Messaging, ID } from 'appwrite';
const account = new Account(client);

// 1. Register device token as target
const target = await account.createPushTarget({
  targetId: ID.unique(),
  identifier: '<FCM_OR_APNS_DEVICE_TOKEN>',
  providerId: '<PROVIDER_ID_FROM_STEP_1>' // FCM/APNS provider ID
});

// 2. Subscribe target to topic
const messagingClient = new Messaging(client);
await messagingClient.createSubscriber({
  topicId: 'announcements',
  subscriberId: ID.unique(),
  targetId: target.$id
});
```

### 2. Send Push to All Subscribers (Server-side only)

```typescript
import { ID } from 'node-appwrite';

await messaging.createPush({
  messageId: ID.unique(),
  title: 'New Update!',
  body: 'Hello subscribers',
  topics: ['announcements'], // <- all targets subscribed to this topic
  // optional: data, action, image, sound, badge, draft, scheduledAt
  // data: { deepLink: 'myapp://home' },
  // draft: false
});
```

This creates a `Messaging > Messages > Push` message targeting `topics: ['announcements']`. Appwrite fans out via the configured FCM/APNS provider to every `target` subscribed to that topic.

You can also do this from Console: `Messaging > Create Message > Push > Topics > announcements > Send`.

> Requires Appwrite >=1.5 with Messaging enabled and server API Key with `messages.write` + `topics.write` scopes. Users without a valid `pushTarget` or subscription will not receive it.

Using the Web SDK (`appwrite`), subscribe to both channels in a single `realtime.subscribe()` call and branch on `response.channels` / `response.events`:

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const DATABASE_ID = '[DATABASE_ID]';
const TABLE_ID = '[TABLE_ID]';
const BUCKET_ID = '[BUCKET_ID]';

const client = new Client()
  .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
  .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

// Subscribe to table rows AND bucket files simultaneously
const subscription = await realtime.subscribe([
  Channel.tablesdb(DATABASE_ID).table(TABLE_ID).row(), // -> tablesdb.[DB_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows
  Channel.bucket(BUCKET_ID).file() // -> buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files
], (response) => {
  // response = { events: string[], payload: any, channels: string[], timestamp: string }

  const isRowEvent = response.channels.some(c => c.startsWith(`tablesdb.${DATABASE_ID}`));
  const isFileEvent = response.channels.some(c => c.startsWith(`buckets.${BUCKET_ID}`));

  if (isRowEvent) {
    // payload is the row document
    if (response.events.some(e => e.includes('.create'))) {
      console.log('Row created:', response.payload);
    } else if (response.events.some(e => e.includes('.update'))) {
      console.log('Row updated:', response.payload);
    } else if (response.events.some(e => e.includes('.delete'))) {
      console.log('Row deleted:', response.payload);
    }
    console.log('Row event:', response.events, response.channels);
  }

  if (isFileEvent) {
    // payload is Models.File - only handle uploads (create)
    if (response.events.some(e => e.includes('.create'))) {
      console.log('File uploaded:', response.payload);
      // response.payload.name, response.payload.mimeType, etc.
    } else if (response.events.some(e => e.includes('.delete'))) {
      console.log('File deleted:', response.payload);
    }
    console.log('File event:', response.events, response.channels);
  }
});

// Unsubscribe when done (e.g. on component unmount)
// await subscription.close();
```

