Here's a complete email/password signup and login flow with the Appwrite Web SDK.

### Install

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

### Client setup

```typescript
import { Client, Account, ID } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const account = new Account(client);
```

Replace `<REGION>` (e.g. `nyc`, `fra`) and `[PROJECT_ID]` with your project values.

### Signup and login

```typescript
async function signUp(email: string, password: string, name?: string) {
    const user = await account.create({
        userId: ID.unique(),
        email,
        password,
        name,
    });

    // Creating an account does not start a session — log in next
    const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
        email,
        password,
    });

    return { user, session };
}

async function logIn(email: string, password: string) {
    const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
        email,
        password,
    });
    return session;
}

async function getCurrentUser() {
    return await account.get();
}

async function logOut() {
    await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
}
```

### Usage

```typescript
// Register, then start a session
await signUp('user@example.com', 'password123', 'Ada Lovelace');

// Or sign in an existing user
await logIn('user@example.com', 'password123');

const user = await getCurrentUser();
console.log(user.$id, user.email, user.name);

await logOut();
```

`account.create` only creates the user. You still need `createEmailPasswordSession` to establish a session (the SDK stores it in a cookie automatically in the browser). Password must be at least 8 characters.

Creating a database and table is an admin operation (API key). The Web SDK (`appwrite`) can call the same TablesDB methods, but do not put an API key in browser code — use `node-appwrite` on the server for schema setup, then `createRow` from the client.

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, ID } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

// 1. Create database (admin / server-side only)
const database = await tablesDB.create({
    databaseId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'App Database',
});

// 2. Create "tasks" table with columns (admin / server-side only)
const table = await tablesDB.createTable({
    databaseId: database.$id,
    tableId: 'tasks',
    name: 'tasks',
    columns: [
        { key: 'title', type: 'varchar', size: 255, required: true },
        { key: 'completed', type: 'boolean', required: true },
        { key: 'dueDate', type: 'datetime', required: false },
    ],
});

// 3. Insert a row
const row = await tablesDB.createRow({
    databaseId: database.$id,
    tableId: table.$id,
    rowId: ID.unique(),
    data: {
        title: 'Ship TablesDB example',
        completed: false,
        dueDate: new Date('2026-04-01T17:00:00.000Z').toISOString(),
    },
});

console.log(row.$id, row.title, row.completed, row.dueDate);
```

On the server, swap the import and authenticate with a key:

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID!)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY!);
```

`dueDate` is stored as an ISO 8601 string. `varchar` needs a `size` (max 16383); keep it small for titles so the column stays fully indexable.

Use the `Query` class with `TablesDB.listRows()`. Pass an array of query strings in the `queries` option. Multiple queries are **AND**ed together by default.

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite';
// Server-side: import from 'node-appwrite'

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.equal('status', 'active'),
        Query.limit(10),
    ],
});

// results.total — total matching rows
// results.rows  — the page of rows
```

## Filtering

```typescript
// Equality (pass an array for IN)
Query.equal('status', 'active')
Query.equal('status', ['active', 'pending'])
Query.notEqual('role', 'guest')

// Comparisons
Query.lessThan('price', 100)
Query.lessThanEqual('price', 100)
Query.greaterThan('views', 1000)
Query.greaterThanEqual('rating', 4)
Query.between('age', 18, 65)

// Null checks
Query.isNull('deletedAt')
Query.isNotNull('publishedAt')

// Strings
Query.startsWith('email', 'admin@')
Query.endsWith('slug', '-draft')
Query.contains('tags', 'typescript')   // string or array column
Query.search('body', 'appwrite query') // full-text; needs a full-text index

// Logical
Query.or([
    Query.equal('status', 'draft'),
    Query.equal('status', 'review'),
])
Query.and([
    Query.greaterThan('age', 18),
    Query.lessThan('age', 65),
])
```

## Sorting

```typescript
Query.orderAsc('createdAt')
Query.orderDesc('$createdAt') // built-in timestamp
```

You can combine multiple order queries; they apply in the order you pass them.

## Limiting and selecting fields

```typescript
Query.limit(25)                    // default 25, max 100
Query.select(['title', 'status'])  // return only these columns (+ system fields)
```

## Pagination

**Offset** — simple, but slower on large tables:

```typescript
const page = 2;
const pageSize = 25;

const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.equal('status', 'active'),
        Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'),
        Query.limit(pageSize),
        Query.offset((page - 1) * pageSize),
    ],
});
```

**Cursor** — preferred for large datasets (stable, no skipped/duplicated rows):

```typescript
// First page
const first = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'),
        Query.limit(25),
    ],
});

// Next page — after the last row of the current page
const next = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'),
        Query.limit(25),
        Query.cursorAfter(first.rows[first.rows.length - 1].$id),
    ],
});

// Previous page
const prev = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'),
        Query.limit(25),
        Query.cursorBefore(next.rows[0].$id),
    ],
});
```

## Putting it together

```typescript
const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.equal('status', 'published'),
        Query.greaterThan('views', 100),
        Query.search('title', 'appwrite'),
        Query.or([
            Query.equal('category', 'guides'),
            Query.equal('featured', true),
        ]),
        Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'),
        Query.limit(20),
        Query.select(['title', 'slug', 'views', 'category']),
    ],
});

for (const row of results.rows) {
    console.log(row.$id, row.title);
}
```

**Notes**

- Queries are AND by default; wrap alternatives in `Query.or([...])`.
- `Query.limit` max is **100**. Use cursor pagination to walk larger result sets.
- `Query.search` requires a **full-text index** on that column.
- Equality / comparison filters on a column typically need an index for good performance.
- Prefer `cursorAfter` / `cursorBefore` over `offset` once tables get large.

Use `Query.greaterThan`, `Query.orderDesc`, and `Query.limit` together on `listRows`. Queries in the array are combined with AND.

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite';
// Server-side: import from 'node-appwrite'

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.greaterThan('amount', 100), // amount > 100
        Query.orderDesc('amount'),        // highest first
        Query.limit(20),                  // at most 20 rows
    ],
});
```

- `Query.greaterThan('amount', 100)` — numeric column strictly greater than 100. Use `Query.greaterThanEqual` if you want `>= 100`.
- `Query.orderDesc('amount')` — sort that same field descending.
- `Query.limit(20)` — cap the page size (default is 25, max is 100).

Replace `'amount'` with your column key. The column should be a numeric type (`integer` or `float`) and typically indexed if you filter/sort on it at scale.

Use the Node.js runtime’s default export: an async handler that receives `{ req, res, log, error }`. Read the payload from `req.bodyJson` and return JSON with `res.json()`.

```js
// src/main.js
export default async ({ req, res, log, error }) => {
    const data = req.bodyJson;

    if (!data?.name) {
        error('Missing name field');
        return res.json({ error: 'Name is required' }, 400);
    }

    const greeting = `Hello, ${data.name}!`;
    log(`Greeting ${data.name}`);

    return res.json({ greeting });
};
```

**Signature:** `export default async ({ req, res, log, error }) => { ... }`

**Request:** `req.bodyJson` is the parsed JSON body (or `undefined` if it is not JSON).

**Response:** `res.json(body, status?)` sets `Content-Type: application/json`. Other helpers: `res.text()`, `res.empty()`, `res.redirect()`, `res.send(body, status, headers)`.

**Example request**

```json
{ "name": "Ada" }
```

**Example response** (`200`)

```json
{ "greeting": "Hello, Ada!" }
```

If `name` is missing, the function returns `400` with `{ "error": "Name is required" }`.

Bucket creation is an **admin** operation. The Web SDK (`appwrite`) cannot create buckets — that needs an API key, which must never live in the browser. Create the bucket with the **Server SDK**, then upload and preview with the **Web SDK**.

## 1. Create the bucket (Server SDK)

```typescript
import {
    Client,
    Storage,
    ID,
    Permission,
    Role,
    Compression,
} from 'node-appwrite';

const admin = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID!)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY!); // never expose this in the browser

const storage = new Storage(admin);

const bucket = await storage.createBucket({
    bucketId: ID.unique(), // or a fixed id like 'images'
    name: 'images',
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.any()),      // anyone can read (needed for public previews)
        Permission.create(Role.users()),  // signed-in users can upload
        Permission.update(Role.users()),
        Permission.delete(Role.users()),
    ],
    fileSecurity: true, // also enforce per-file permissions
    enabled: true,
    maximumFileSize: 10 * 1024 * 1024, // 10 MB
    allowedFileExtensions: ['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'gif', 'webp'],
    compression: Compression.Gzip,
    encryption: false, // keep off if you rely on image transforms
    antivirus: true,
});

console.log('Bucket ID:', bucket.$id);
```

`fileSecurity: true` means **both** bucket and file permissions must allow the action.

## 2. Upload + preview (Web SDK)

```typescript
import {
    Client,
    Storage,
    Account,
    ID,
    Permission,
    Role,
    AppwriteException,
} from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const account = new Account(client);
const storage = new Storage(client);

const BUCKET_ID = '<BUCKET_ID>'; // from createBucket

async function uploadImage(file: File) {
    const user = await account.get(); // must be signed in to create files

    const uploaded = await storage.createFile({
        bucketId: BUCKET_ID,
        fileId: ID.unique(),
        file,
        permissions: [
            Permission.read(Role.any()),           // public preview URL
            Permission.update(Role.user(user.$id)),
            Permission.delete(Role.user(user.$id)),
        ],
    });

    // getFilePreview returns a URL string (no network request)
    const previewUrl = storage.getFilePreview({
        bucketId: BUCKET_ID,
        fileId: uploaded.$id,
        width: 400,
        height: 400,
        quality: 80,
        output: 'webp',
    });

    return { fileId: uploaded.$id, previewUrl: previewUrl.toString() };
}

// Example: <input type="file" accept="image/*" id="image" />
document.getElementById('image')?.addEventListener('change', async (e) => {
    const file = (e.target as HTMLInputElement).files?.[0];
    if (!file) return;

    try {
        const { fileId, previewUrl } = await uploadImage(file);
        const img = document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>('#preview')!;
        img.src = previewUrl;
        console.log('Uploaded', fileId, previewUrl);
    } catch (err) {
        if (err instanceof AppwriteException) {
            console.error(err.code, err.type, err.message);
        } else {
            throw err;
        }
    }
});
```

## Permissions recap

| Level | What it controls |
|---|---|
| Bucket `Permission.create(Role.users())` | Who may upload |
| Bucket `Permission.read(Role.any())` | Who may read/preview (when `fileSecurity` is on, file perms also apply) |
| File `Permission.read(Role.any())` | Public preview/download of this file |
| File `Permission.update/delete(Role.user(id))` | Only the owner can change or remove it |

Do **not** use `Permission.write(Role.any())` — that lets guests overwrite and delete files.

For a private image, use `Permission.read(Role.user(user.$id))` instead of `Role.any()`. The preview URL then only works for that signed-in user (or with a file token).

Appwrite **Messaging** sends push notifications through configured providers. You register device tokens as **targets**, optionally group them with **topics**, then send from the server SDK (API key). Clients never send pushes themselves.

## Supported push providers

| Provider | Platforms | What you need |
|----------|-----------|----------------|
| **FCM** (Firebase Cloud Messaging) | Android, web, and iOS (via FCM) | Firebase service account JSON |
| **APNs** (Apple Push Notification service) | iOS, iPadOS, macOS | `.p8` auth key, Key ID, Team ID, Bundle ID |

Email/SMS providers (SMTP, Mailgun, SendGrid, Twilio, etc.) are separate; they are not used for push.

---

## 1. Create a provider

**Console:** Messaging → Providers → Create provider → FCM or APNs.

**API (server SDK):**

```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

// FCM
await messaging.createFCMProvider({
    providerId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'FCM',
    serviceAccountJSON: {
        // Firebase service account JSON
        type: 'service_account',
        project_id: '...',
        private_key: '...',
        client_email: '...',
        // ...
    },
});

// APNs
await messaging.createAPNSProvider({
    providerId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'APNs',
    authKey: '<contents of AuthKey_XXXXXXXX.p8>',
    authKeyId: '<KEY_ID>',
    teamId: '<APPLE_TEAM_ID>',
    bundleId: 'com.example.app',
    sandbox: true, // true for development, false for production
});
```

API keys need the **Messaging** scope.

---

## 2. Register a device target

Get the FCM/APNs device token from the OS (Firebase Messaging, APNs, or a wrapper like Notifee / `expo-notifications`). Then attach it to the logged-in user.

**Client SDK** (user must be signed in):

```typescript
import { Account, ID } from 'appwrite';

const account = new Account(client);

const target = await account.createPushTarget({
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    identifier: deviceToken,      // FCM or APNs token
    providerId: '<PROVIDER_ID>',  // optional; Appwrite can pick a matching provider
});

// Persist target.$id — you need it when the token rotates
await account.updatePushTarget({
    targetId: target.$id,
    identifier: newDeviceToken,
});
```

**Server SDK** (admin, any user):

```typescript
import { Users, MessagingProviderType, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const users = new Users(client);

await users.createTarget({
    userId: '<USER_ID>',
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    providerType: MessagingProviderType.Push,
    identifier: deviceToken,
    providerId: '<PROVIDER_ID>', // optional
});
```

Without a target, there is nowhere to deliver the push.

---

## 3. Topics and subscribers

Topics are named audiences (`news`, `orders`, `promo`). A **subscriber** is a target subscribed to a topic.

```typescript
await messaging.createTopic({
    topicId: 'news',
    name: 'News',
    // optional: who may subscribe (default is typically users)
    // subscribe: ['users']
});

await messaging.createSubscriber({
    topicId: 'news',
    subscriberId: ID.unique(),
    targetId: '<TARGET_ID>',
});
```

You can also subscribe from the Console (Messaging → Topics → Subscribers).

---

## 4. Send a push to a topic

Use `createPush` and pass `topics`. This is a **server-side** call.

```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID, MessagePriority } from 'node-appwrite';

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

const message = await messaging.createPush({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    title: 'Weekly digest',
    body: 'Here is what you missed.',
    topics: ['news'], // topic IDs — can pass several
    // optional extras:
    data: { screen: 'digest', id: '42' },
    action: 'appwrite-callback://digest/42',
    image: '<FILE_ID>',   // image from Appwrite Storage
    icon: 'notification_icon',
    sound: 'default',
    color: '#5381D0',
    tag: 'digest',
    badge: 1,
    priority: MessagePriority.High,
    // draft: true,                    // save without sending
    // scheduledAt: '2026-04-01T09:00:00.000Z',
});
```

Appwrite fans the message out to every **push target** subscribed to those topics, using the matching FCM/APNs provider.

### Other recipient modes

You can mix or replace `topics`:

```typescript
await messaging.createPush({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    title: 'Hello',
    body: 'Direct send',
    users: ['<USER_ID>'],       // all of that user's push targets
    targets: ['<TARGET_ID>'],   // one device
});
```

At least one of `topics`, `users`, or `targets` is required (unless `draft: true`).

### Draft / schedule / send later

- `draft: true` — store only; send later with `updatePush` (`draft: false`)
- `scheduledAt` — ISO 8601 time to send
- Console: Messaging → Messages also supports compose / schedule / logs

---

## Typical end-to-end flow

1. Create FCM and/or APNs provider.
2. On the device, obtain a push token and call `account.createPushTarget`.
3. Create topics; subscribe each target (`createSubscriber`).
4. From a Function, backend, or the Console, call `messaging.createPush({ topics: ['news'], title, body })`.
5. On token refresh, `updatePushTarget`; on logout/uninstall, `deletePushTarget`.

**Notes**

- Sending requires a server SDK + API key (or the Console). The web/React Native client cannot create messages.
- iOS can use **APNs directly** or **FCM** (FCM forwards to APNs). Android/web use FCM.
- `data` is a custom key/value payload for the app; `title`/`body` are the visible notification.
- Check Messaging → Messages for delivery status and provider errors (invalid token, sandbox mismatch, etc.).

A **push provider** must be configured first. Appwrite cannot deliver push messages until at least one of these is set up in the project (Console → **Messaging → Providers**, or the Messaging API):

- **Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM)** — Android and typically also web / Flutter
- **Apple Push Notification service (APNs)** — iOS / macOS

You also need an API key with Messaging scopes if you send from the server SDK.

After that, the flow is: **provider → user push targets → topic → subscribers → message**.

### 1. Register device tokens as push targets

Each device must have a push target on the user (the FCM/APNs token). Client-side, after you obtain the token from the OS/SDK:

```typescript
import { Account, ID } from 'appwrite';

const account = new Account(client);

await account.createPushTarget({
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    identifier: devicePushToken, // FCM or APNs token
    providerId: '<PROVIDER_ID>', // optional; Appwrite can pick a matching provider
});
```

### 2. Create a topic

Server-side (`node-appwrite`):

```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

await messaging.createTopic({
    topicId: ID.unique(), // or a stable ID like 'announcements'
    name: 'Announcements',
});
```

### 3. Subscribe targets to the topic

Subscriptions are per **target**, not per user. Subscribe every device that should receive the topic:

```typescript
await messaging.createSubscriber({
    topicId: 'announcements',
    subscriberId: ID.unique(),
    targetId: '<TARGET_ID>',
});
```

Anyone subscribed to that topic is now in the audience.

### 4. Send a push to the topic

Pass the topic ID(s). Omit `draft: true` (or set `draft: false`) so the message is sent immediately:

```typescript
await messaging.createPush({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    title: 'New update',
    body: 'Something happened.',
    topics: ['announcements'], // all subscribers to this topic
    // users: [],   // optional extra recipients
    // targets: [], // optional extra devices
});
```

Appwrite fans the message out to every **push target** subscribed to those topics, using the configured FCM/APNs provider.

### Practical notes

- **No provider → no delivery.** Topics and messages can exist, but nothing is sent until FCM/APNs credentials are valid.
- **No targets / no subscribers → empty send.** Creating a topic is not enough; devices must have targets and those targets must be subscribed.
- You can also target `users` or `targets` on `createPush`; using only `topics` is how you reach *all* subscribers of that topic.
- Use `draft: true` to save the message, then update/send later; use `scheduledAt` for a future send.
- Sending is a **server** operation (API key). Clients register tokens and (if you expose it) can manage their own targets; they do not broadcast to a whole topic.

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(),
        Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file(),
    ],
    (response) => {
        const events = response.events as string[];

        if (events.some((e) => e.includes('tablesdb') && e.includes('rows'))) {
            // Table row create / update / delete
            if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.create'))) {
                console.log('Row created:', response.payload);
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.update'))) {
                console.log('Row updated:', response.payload);
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.delete'))) {
                console.log('Row deleted:', response.payload);
            }
        } else if (events.some((e) => e.includes('buckets') && e.includes('files'))) {
            // File create / update / delete (uploads land as .create)
            if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.create'))) {
                console.log('File uploaded:', response.payload);
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.update'))) {
                console.log('File updated:', response.payload);
            } else if (events.some((e) => e.endsWith('.delete'))) {
                console.log('File deleted:', response.payload);
            }
        }
    }
);

// Later: await subscription.close();
```

Replace `[PROJECT_ID]`, `[DATABASE_ID]`, `[TABLE_ID]`, `[BUCKET_ID]`, and the regional endpoint. The user must be authenticated (or the table/bucket must allow guest read) for events to arrive. `response.payload` is the row or file; `response.events` looks like `tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.create` or `buckets.*.files.*.create`.

Use one `subscribe()` with both channels:

```typescript
import { Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(),
        Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file(),
    ],
    (response) => {
        console.log(response.events);
        console.log(response.payload);
    }
);
```

Exact channel strings:

- Table rows: `tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows`
- Bucket files: `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files`

You can pass those strings instead of the `Channel` helpers:

```typescript
await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        'tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows',
        'buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files',
    ],
    (response) => { /* ... */ }
);
```

# Email/Password Authentication with Appwrite Web SDK

## Installation

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

## Implementation

```typescript
// appwrite.ts
import { Client, Account, ID, AppwriteException } from 'appwrite';

// 1. Set up the client
const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your Appwrite endpoint
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');                          // Your project ID

const account = new Account(client);

// 2. Create an account (signup)
async function signup(email: string, password: string, name: string) {
    try {
        const user = await account.create({
            userId: ID.unique(),
            email: email,
            password: password,
            name: name // optional
        });
        console.log('Account created:', user);
        return user;
    } catch (err) {
        if (err instanceof AppwriteException) {
            console.error(`Signup failed [${err.code}]: ${err.message}`);
            // e.g. 409 if a user with this email already exists
        }
        throw err;
    }
}

// 3. Create a session (login)
async function login(email: string, password: string) {
    try {
        const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
            email: email,
            password: password
        });
        console.log('Logged in:', session);
        return session;
    } catch (err) {
        if (err instanceof AppwriteException) {
            console.error(`Login failed [${err.code}]: ${err.message}`);
            // e.g. 401 if credentials are invalid
        }
        throw err;
    }
}

// Get the currently logged-in user
async function getCurrentUser() {
    try {
        return await account.get();
    } catch (err) {
        // Throws 401 if there's no active session
        return null;
    }
}

// Logout
async function logout() {
    await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
}
```

## Usage

```typescript
// Signup — typically followed by login to create a session right away
await signup('user@example.com', 'supersecret123', 'Jane Doe');
await login('user@example.com', 'supersecret123');

// Or login an existing user
await login('user@example.com', 'supersecret123');

// Check who's logged in
const user = await getCurrentUser();
console.log(user?.email);
```

## Key Points

- **One session at a time** — calling `createEmailPasswordSession` while a session exists throws an error. Call `logout()` (or delete the specific session) first, or check for an existing session with `account.get()`.
- **`ID.unique()`** auto-generates a user ID; you can also pass your own.
- **Password requirements** — Appwrite requires a minimum of 8 characters by default (configurable in the console under Auth settings).
- **Error handling** — use `AppwriteException` to inspect `code`, `type`, and `message` (401 = invalid credentials, 409 = email already registered).
- **Region** — replace `<REGION>` with your Appwrite Cloud region (e.g., `nyc`, `fra`) or your self-hosted endpoint.

# Appwrite Sessions

A **session** represents an authenticated user's connection to your project. Once a session exists, the SDK includes its credentials with every request so Appwrite knows which user is calling.

## Creating Sessions

Sessions are created through the `Account` service (client-side) with any of these methods:

```typescript
// Email + password
const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({
    email: 'user@example.com',
    password: 'password123'
});

// OAuth2 (web — redirects the browser)
account.createOAuth2Session({
    provider: OAuthProvider.Github,
    success: 'https://example.com/success',
    failure: 'https://example.com/fail',
});

// Other methods (same pattern):
// account.createAnonymousSession()
// account.createMagicURLToken() + account.createSession({ userId, secret })
// account.createPhoneToken() / createEmailToken() + account.createSession()
```

**How credentials are stored:**
- **Web:** The SDK stores the session in a cookie automatically (on Appwrite Cloud, it's a secure, HttpOnly-style cookie scoped to the endpoint).
- **React Native / mobile:** Stored in device storage by the SDK.
- **SSR (Next.js, etc.):** Your server creates the session with an **admin client**, then stores `session.secret` in your own cookie (which **must** be named `a_session_<PROJECT_ID>`), and passes it to a **session client** per request:

```typescript
const sessionClient = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .setSession(req.cookies['a_session_[PROJECT_ID]']);
```

## Managing Sessions

```typescript
// Check if logged in / get current user
const user = await account.get();            // throws 401 if no valid session

// List all active sessions for the user
const sessions = await account.listSessions();  // includes device, IP, OS info

// Get a specific session
const session = await account.getSession({ sessionId: '[SESSION_ID]' });

// Extend/refresh a session (renew its expiry)
await account.updateSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
```

Each session tracks useful metadata: `expire` timestamp, `provider`, `ip`, `osName`, `clientName`, `countryName`, and whether it's `current`.

## Deleting Sessions

```typescript
// Logout — delete the current session
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });

// Delete a specific session (e.g., "log out my old phone")
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: '[SESSION_ID]' });

// Logout everywhere — delete ALL sessions
await account.deleteSessions();
```

Server-side, the `Users` service gives admins control:

```typescript
const users = new Users(client);
await users.listSessions({ userId: '[USER_ID]' });
await users.deleteSession({ userId: '[USER_ID]', sessionId: '[SESSION_ID]' });
await users.deleteSessions({ userId: '[USER_ID]' }); // force-logout a user
```

## Session Limits & Expiration

- **Concurrent session limit:** By default, a user can have up to **10 active sessions**. When an 11th session is created, Appwrite automatically **deletes the oldest session** (FIFO eviction) — users don't get an error, they just get logged out on their oldest device.
- **Expiration:** Sessions expire after **1 year (365 days)** by default. After expiry, requests return `401` and the user must log in again. You can renew a session before it expires with `updateSession()`.
- **Self-hosting:** Both limits are configurable via environment variables — the session limit via `_APP_AUTH_SESSIONS_LIMIT` and the duration via `_APP_AUTH_DURATION`.
- **Rate limits:** Session-creation endpoints are rate-limited per IP/user to prevent brute-force attacks (returns `429`); server-side admin clients with API keys bypass these.

**Practical tips:**
- After logout, don't just clear local state — actually call `deleteSession()` so the server-side session is invalidated.
- Use `listSessions()` to build a "manage devices" screen so users can revoke sessions remotely.
- Deleting a user (`users.delete()`) also deletes all their sessions automatically.

Here's the complete code. One important note up front: **creating databases, tables, and columns requires an API key**, so those steps run with the server SDK (`node-appwrite`) — in a script, CI job, or Appwrite Function. Inserting rows can be done from either the Web SDK or server SDK.

```bash
npm install node-appwrite
```

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://fra.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // your Appwrite region
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY); // never expose this key in browser code

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

// 1. Create the database
const database = await tablesDB.create({
    databaseId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'Tasks Database',
});

// 2. Create the table with columns inline
const table = await tablesDB.createTable({
    databaseId: database.$id,
    tableId: 'tasks',
    name: 'Tasks',
    columns: [
        { key: 'title',     type: 'varchar',  size: 255, required: true  },
        { key: 'completed', type: 'boolean',  required: true  },
        { key: 'dueDate',   type: 'datetime', required: false },
    ],
});

// 3. Insert a row
const row = await tablesDB.createRow({
    databaseId: database.$id,
    tableId: 'tasks',
    rowId: ID.unique(),
    data: {
        title: 'Write Appwrite tutorial',
        completed: false,
        dueDate: '2025-12-31T17:00:00.000Z', // ISO 8601 format
    },
});

console.log('Row created:', row.$id);
```

### Client-side equivalent (Web SDK) for row insertion

Once the table exists, browser apps use the `appwrite` package with a normal session client:

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, ID } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://fra.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const row = await tablesDB.createRow({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'tasks',
    rowId: ID.unique(),
    data: {
        title: 'Ship the feature',
        completed: false,
        dueDate: new Date('2025-12-31T17:00:00Z').toISOString(),
    },
});
```

Key points:

- `varchar` requires a `size` (max 768 if you want to index it; 255 is a common default) and counts against the 64 KB inline row limit. For longer text, use `text`/`mediumtext`/`longtext` instead.
- `datetime` values must be ISO 8601 strings.
- If the table's permissions aren't permissive, client-side row creation needs either table-level create permissions or explicit row-level `permissions` (e.g., `Permission.read(Role.user('[USER_ID]'))`).
- Row creation from the browser requires the user to be authenticated (or an anonymous session) unless the table allows guest access.

# Querying Rows in Appwrite TablesDB

You query rows with `tablesDB.listRows()`, passing an array of `Query` builder strings in the `queries` parameter. **Multiple queries are combined with AND logic by default.**

## Basic Setup

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite';
// Server-side: import from 'node-appwrite'

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);
```

## Filtering

```typescript
// Equality
Query.equal('status', 'active')                  // single value
Query.equal('status', ['active', 'pending'])     // IN — matches any value

// Comparison
Query.notEqual('status', 'archived')
Query.lessThan('price', 100)
Query.lessThanEqual('price', 100)
Query.greaterThan('age', 18)
Query.greaterThanEqual('age', 18)
Query.between('price', 10, 50)                   // 10 <= price <= 50

// Null checks
Query.isNull('deletedAt')
Query.isNotNull('email')

// String matching
Query.startsWith('title', 'Intro')
Query.endsWith('filename', '.png')
Query.contains('tags', 'typescript')             // substring or array element
Query.search('content', 'full text keywords')    // requires a full-text index
```

Multiple filters in one call (implicit AND):

```typescript
const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.equal('status', 'published'),
        Query.greaterThanEqual('views', 1000),
        Query.isNotNull('publishedAt'),
    ],
});
```

Explicit OR / AND:

```typescript
// OR: posts that are either featured OR have high views
Query.or([
    Query.equal('featured', true),
    Query.greaterThan('views', 10000),
])

// Explicit AND (same as passing queries separately)
Query.and([
    Query.greaterThan('age', 18),
    Query.lessThan('age', 65),
])
```

## Sorting

```typescript
Query.orderAsc('title')        // A → Z
Query.orderDesc('$createdAt')  // newest first
```

You can sort by multiple fields — order matters:

```typescript
queries: [
    Query.orderDesc('priority'),   // highest priority first
    Query.orderAsc('$createdAt'),  // ties broken by oldest first
]
```

## Limiting and Field Selection

```typescript
Query.limit(25)                          // default 25, max 100
Query.select(['title', 'author', 'views'])  // return only these fields
```

## Pagination

### Offset pagination (simple, fine for small datasets)

```typescript
// Page 1
await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.offset(0)],
});

// Page 2
await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [Query.limit(25), Query.offset(25)],
});
```

### Cursor pagination (preferred — better performance and no skipped/duplicated rows when data changes)

```typescript
// Page 1
const page1 = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [Query.limit(25)],
});

// Page 2 — start after the last row of page 1
const lastRow = page1.rows[page1.rows.length - 1];

const page2 = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.limit(25),
        Query.cursorAfter(lastRow.$id),
    ],
});

// cursorBefore() paginates in the opposite direction
Query.cursorBefore('[ROW_ID]')
```

## Complete Example

```typescript
const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.equal('status', 'published'),       // filter
        Query.or([                                 // OR condition
            Query.equal('featured', true),
            Query.greaterThan('views', 1000),
        ]),
        Query.orderDesc('$createdAt'),             // sort
        Query.select(['title', 'slug', 'views']),  // limit fields
        Query.limit(10),                           // page size
    ],
});

console.log(results.total);  // total matching rows
console.log(results.rows);   // the row documents
```

## Tips

- **Indexes:** Filtering and sorting on large tables requires indexes on those columns — Appwrite will reject queries that would trigger a full table scan.
- **Sorting with filters:** When you sort by a field you also filter on, the sort field generally needs to appear in the same index.
- **Cursor over offset:** Use `cursorAfter` for infinite scroll or large tables; offset queries get slower as the offset grows and can miss rows when data is inserted mid-pagination.

You can combine three query methods in the `queries` array of `listRows`:

- `Query.greaterThan('field', 100)` — filter rows where the field is > 100
- `Query.orderDesc('field')` — sort by that field, highest first
- `Query.limit(20)` — cap results at 20

```typescript
import { Client, TablesDB, Query } from 'appwrite';
// Server-side: import from 'node-appwrite'

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

const results = await tablesDB.listRows({
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    queries: [
        Query.greaterThan('score', 100),  // score > 100
        Query.orderDesc('score'),         // highest score first
        Query.limit(20),                  // at most 20 rows
    ],
});

console.log(results.total);      // total matching rows
console.log(results.documents);  // the up-to-20 rows
```

A few notes:

- **Multiple queries are ANDed by default**, so all three conditions apply together.
- **Indexing:** For good performance on large tables, create an index on the numeric column (`score`) in the Appwrite Console — it's used for both the filter and the sort.
- **Limit bounds:** `Query.limit()` accepts 1–100; the default is 25 if omitted.
- If you wanted "greater than or equal to 100" instead, use `Query.greaterThanEqual('score', 100)`.

Here's a complete Appwrite Function in Node.js that greets the user by name:

```js
// src/main.js
export default async ({ req, res, log, error }) => {
  log(`${req.method} ${req.path}`);

  // Handle only POST requests with a JSON body
  if (req.method !== 'POST') {
    return res.json({ error: 'Please send a POST request with a JSON body' }, 405);
  }

  // req.bodyJson is the parsed JSON body (undefined if body isn't valid JSON)
  const name = req.bodyJson?.name;

  if (!name || typeof name !== 'string') {
    error('Missing or invalid "name" field in request body');
    return res.json({ error: 'The "name" field is required' }, 400);
  }

  const greeting = `Hello, ${name}! Welcome to Appwrite Functions. 🚀`;

  log(`Greeting generated for: ${name}`);

  return res.json({
    success: true,
    greeting,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
  });
};
```

### Key points

- **Signature**: The entry file must `export default` an async function receiving the context object `{ req, res, log, error }`.
- **Body parsing**: Use `req.bodyJson` — Appwrite automatically parses JSON request bodies. It will be `undefined` if the body isn't valid JSON, hence the optional chaining.
- **Responses**: Return `res.json(data, statusCode)` — it sets the `Content-Type: application/json` header automatically. Other options include `res.text()`, `res.send()`, `res.empty()`, and `res.redirect()`.
- **Logging**: Use `log()` for general output and `error()` for errors — both show up in the function's execution logs in the console.

### Testing it

```bash
curl -X POST https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/functions/<FUNCTION_ID>/executions \
  -H "X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"name": "Ada"}'
```

Or via the SDK:

```js
const functions = new Functions(client);
const execution = await functions.createExecution({
  functionId: '<FUNCTION_ID>',
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Ada' }),
});
```

**Example response:**
```json
{
  "success": true,
  "greeting": "Hello, Ada! Welcome to Appwrite Functions. 🚀",
  "timestamp": "2025-01-15T12:00:00.000Z"
}
```

# Create Bucket, Upload File, and Get Image Preview

**Important note:** Creating a bucket requires an API key, so it must be done **server-side** using `node-appwrite` (browsers should never hold API keys). Uploading files and generating preview URLs can be done client-side with the Web SDK. Here's both parts:

## 1. Create the Bucket (Server-side only)

```typescript
// server.ts — run in Node.js (backend, serverless function, or script)
import { Client, Storage, ID, Permission, Role } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY!); // API key with storage scope

const storage = new Storage(client);

const bucket = await storage.createBucket({
    bucketId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'User Images',
    permissions: [
        Permission.create(Role.users()),  // any logged-in user can upload
        Permission.read(Role.any()),      // anyone can view files
        Permission.update(Role.users()),  // logged-in users can update
        Permission.delete(Role.users()),  // logged-in users can delete
    ],
    fileSecurity: true,                    // enforce per-file permissions
    allowedFileExtensions: ['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'webp'],
    maximumFileSize: 5 * 1024 * 1024,     // 5 MB limit
    enabled: true,
});

console.log('Bucket created:', bucket.$id);
```

> With `fileSecurity: true`, permissions set on individual files take precedence. With it off, the bucket-level permissions apply to all files.

## 2. Upload a File and Get a Preview URL (Web SDK / Browser)

```typescript
// client.ts — browser code
import { Client, Storage, ID, Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const storage = new Storage(client);

// Get the file from an <input type="file"> element
const fileInput = document.getElementById('file-input') as HTMLInputElement;

const uploadedFile = await storage.createFile({
    bucketId: '[BUCKET_ID]',
    fileId: ID.unique(),
    file: fileInput.files![0],
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.any()),                    // publicly viewable
        Permission.update(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),      // only owner can update
        Permission.delete(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),      // only owner can delete
    ],
});

console.log('Uploaded:', uploadedFile.$id);

// Get a preview URL (resized/cropped image served via CDN)
const previewUrl = storage.getFilePreview({
    bucketId: '[BUCKET_ID]',
    fileId: uploadedFile.$id,
    width: 400,       // optional: resize width (px)
    height: 400,      // optional: resize height (px)
    gravity: 'center', // optional: crop focus point
    quality: 80,      // optional: 0–100
    output: 'webp',   // optional: convert format
});

// Use it directly in an <img> tag
document.getElementById('preview')!.setAttribute('src', previewUrl);
```

## Key Points

- **`getFilePreview()` returns a URL synchronously** (no `await` needed) — it just constructs the URL string; the image is processed server-side on request.
- **Preview requires read permission** — if the file isn't readable by the requester, the URL returns a 401. `Role.any()` makes it work in public `<img>` tags.
- **Don't use `Role.any()` with `update`/`delete`** — that would let anonymous guests modify or remove your files.
- Preview options like `width`, `height`, `quality`, `gravity`, and `output` are all optional — call `getFilePreview({ bucketId, fileId })` alone for the full-size image.
- For **private images**, users must be authenticated in the browser session for the preview URL to load, or you can generate a JWT (`account.createJWT()`) and pass it as a query param for short-lived access.

# Setting Up Push Notifications with Appwrite Messaging

## Overview

Push notifications in Appwrite flow through four components:

1. **Provider** — the third-party service that actually delivers the notification (e.g., Firebase)
2. **Target** — a specific device belonging to a user (identified by its push token)
3. **Topic** — a channel that users subscribe to for grouped notifications
4. **Message** — the notification you send to targets, topics, or users

## Supported Push Providers

Appwrite Messaging supports the two major push notification providers:

- **Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM)** — for Android, Web, and (optionally) iOS
- **Apple Push Notification Service (APNS)** — for iOS/macOS devices

## Step 1: Configure a Provider

In the Appwrite Console, go to **Messaging → Providers → Create provider**.

**For FCM:**
- Select Firebase Cloud Messaging
- Provide your Firebase service account JSON key (from Firebase Console → Project Settings → Service Accounts)

**For APNS:**
- Select Apple Push Notification Service
- Provide your APNS auth key (`.p8` file), Key ID, Team ID, and Bundle ID, and choose sandbox or production

## Step 2: Register Device Targets (client-side)

Each logged-in user must register their device's push token so Appwrite knows where to send messages:

```typescript
import { Client, Account, ID } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const account = new Account(client);

// After getting the FCM token from the device (e.g., via Firebase SDK)
const fcmToken = await getDevicePushToken(); // platform-specific

await account.createPushTarget({
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    identifier: fcmToken,
    providerId: '[PROVIDER_ID]' // optional if a default provider is set
});
```

> Note: Push targets are tied to user sessions. If the user logs out, remember to call `account.deletePushTarget({ targetId })` to clean up.

## Step 3: Create a Topic and Subscribe Users

Topics let you group subscribers (e.g., "announcements", "sports"). You can create topics in the Console (**Messaging → Topics**) or via the server SDK:

```typescript
// Server-side (node-appwrite)
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

await messaging.createTopic({
    topicId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'Announcements',
    subscribe: ['users'] // who is allowed to subscribe
});
```

Users subscribe to a topic with their target (device):

```typescript
// Client-side
await messaging.createSubscriber({
    topicId: '[TOPIC_ID]',
    subscriberId: ID.unique(),
    targetId: '[TARGET_ID]' // the device target from Step 2
});
```

## Step 4: Send a Push Notification to a Topic

Sending must be done **server-side** (or from the Console, via **Messaging → Messages → Create push notification**):

```typescript
const messaging = new Messaging(client);

await messaging.createPush({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    title: 'New Feature Released! 🚀',
    body: 'Check out the latest update to our app.',
    topics: ['[TOPIC_ID]'],        // send to all subscribers of this topic
    data: { url: '/changelog' },   // optional custom payload
    // Optional extras:
    // icon: 'ic_notification',
    // sound: 'default',
    // color: '#FF0000',
    // action: 'https://example.com',
    // image: 'https://example.com/image.png',
    // draft: false,               // save as draft instead of sending
    // scheduledAt: '2025-01-01T12:00:00Z' // schedule for later
});
```

Instead of (or in addition to) `topics`, you can target:
- `users: ['[USER_ID]']` — all devices of specific users
- `targets: ['[TARGET_ID]']` — specific devices

## Key Things to Remember

- **Client vs. server split:** Registering targets and subscribing to topics is client-side; creating topics (typically) and sending messages is server-side or via Console.
- **Message status:** Track delivery in **Messaging → Messages** — it shows sent/delivered/failed counts per message.
- **APNS vs. FCM:** If you only support Android/Web, FCM alone is enough. For iOS you can use APNS directly or route iOS devices through FCM.
- **Foreground handling:** Appwrite delivers the push, but your app is responsible for how it displays (e.g., Firebase messaging handlers on the client).

# Appwrite Messaging Targeting

Appwrite's **Messaging service** is a server-side-only API (accessed via the Console or a server SDK like `node-appwrite`) for sending email, SMS, and push notifications. It supports three ways to address recipients, and you can mix them in a single message:

## 1. Targeting by Users (`users`)

Pass an array of user IDs. Appwrite looks up every **target** registered on those users that matches the message channel:

- **Push** → all of the user's registered device targets
- **Email** → the user's email address
- **SMS** → the user's phone number

This is convenient because you don't need to track devices yourself — Appwrite resolves each user to their active targets automatically.

## 2. Targeting by Individual Targets (`targets`)

Pass an array of **target IDs** directly. A target is a specific delivery endpoint tied to a user, such as a device (for push) registered via `account.createPushTarget()`. Use this when you want fine-grained control — e.g., notify only a user's phone, not their tablet.

Targets are managed with `users.listTargets()`, `users.createTarget()`, and `users.deleteTarget()` on the server SDK.

## 3. Targeting by Topics (`topics`)

Topics work as publish/subscribe channels. You create a topic (e.g., `"announcements"`), and users **subscribe** their targets to it (client-side with `messaging.createSubscriber()`, or server-side with the Users/Messaging APIs). Sending a message to the topic fans it out to every subscribed target — ideal for broadcasts without enumerating recipients.

## Example (Node.js server SDK)

```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

// Push notification to specific users, targets, and topics (mix and match)
await messaging.createPush({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    title: 'New comment on your post',
    body: 'Alice replied to your thread.',
    users: ['user-1', 'user-2'],           // specific users (all their devices)
    targets: ['target-abc'],               // one specific device
    topics: ['announcements'],             // everyone subscribed to the topic
    data: { postId: '123' },               // optional custom payload
});

// Email to a topic
await messaging.createEmail({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    subject: 'Weekly newsletter',
    content: '<h1>Hello!</h1>',
    topics: ['newsletter'],
    draft: false,                          // set true to send later via console/API
    scheduledAt: '2025-06-01T09:00:00Z',   // optional scheduled delivery
});

// SMS to specific users
await messaging.createSms({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    content: 'Your verification code is 123456',
    users: ['user-1'],
});
```

## Key points

- **At least one of `users`, `targets`, or `topics`** must be provided (unless you're creating a draft to target later).
- **Providers must be configured first** (FCM/APNs for push, SMTP/SendGrid/etc. for email, Twilio/MSG91/Vonage/etc. for SMS) before messages can be delivered on that channel.
- The **status of a message** progresses through `draft` → `scheduled` → `processing` → `sent` (or `failed`), and you can inspect per-target delivery results via `messaging.listTargets()` on the message.
- Duplicate delivery is deduplicated — if a user is addressed both directly and via a topic, they receive the message only once per target.
- Users can **unsubscribe** from topics client-side with `messaging.deleteSubscriber()`, giving you opt-out compliance for broadcasts.

## What must be configured first

Before you can send a push notification to a topic, you need to set up the **Appwrite Messaging service** with a push provider:

1. **Configure a push provider** — In the Appwrite Console (Messaging → Providers), add a provider for your target platform(s):
   - **FCM** (Firebase Cloud Messaging) for Android and Web
   - **APNs** (Apple Push Notification service) for iOS/macOS

   For FCM, you'll upload your Firebase service account credentials; for APNs, you'll configure either a token (.p8 key) or certificate (.p12).

2. **Register users' devices as push targets** — From your client app, each authenticated user's device must register its device token:

```typescript
import { Account } from 'appwrite';

const account = new Account(client);

// Obtain the FCM/APNs device token first, then:
await account.createPushTarget({
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    identifier: deviceToken,
    providerId: '[PROVIDER_ID]' // optional if default provider set
});
```

3. **Create a topic** — Server-side or via the Console:

```typescript
import { Messaging } from 'node-appwrite';

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

const topic = await messaging.createTopic({
    topicId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'Announcements'
});
```

4. **Subscribe targets to the topic** — Each user's target must become a subscriber:

```typescript
// Client-side (or server-side)
await messaging.createSubscriber({
    topicId: '[TOPIC_ID]',
    subscriberId: ID.unique(),
    targetId: '[TARGET_ID]'
});
```

## Sending the push notification

Sending is a **server-side operation** (or done from the Console) — client SDKs can only subscribe, not send. From a backend or Appwrite Function:

```typescript
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY); // API key needs messaging scope

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

await messaging.createPush({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    title: 'New update available!',
    body: 'Check out the latest features.',
    topics: ['[TOPIC_ID]'], // targets every subscriber of this topic
    data: { route: '/updates' } // optional custom payload
});
```

You can optionally pass `scheduledAt` to deliver it later, or track delivery status in the Console under Messaging → Messages.

**Key gotchas:**
- The API key must include the **Messaging scope**.
- Without a registered target (device token), a user won't receive anything even if subscribed.
- A subscriber is tied to a *target*, not the user directly — so multi-device users should register each device and subscribe all of them to the topic.

Here's a complete example using the Appwrite Web SDK to subscribe to both table row changes and file uploads in a single subscription, with different handling for each event type:

```typescript
import { Client, Realtime, Channel, Models } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

// Define your row shape for typed payloads
interface Todo {
    title: string;
    done: boolean;
}

// Subscribe to BOTH channels at once
const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(), // all rows in the table
        Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file(),                        // all files in the bucket
    ],
    (response) => {
        // Route events based on which channel they came from
        const event = response.events[0];

        if (event.startsWith('tablesdb.')) {
            handleRowEvent(event, response.payload as Models.Document & Todo);
        } else if (event.startsWith('buckets.')) {
            handleFileEvent(event, response.payload as Models.File);
        }
    }
);

// --- Row event handler ---
function handleRowEvent(event: string, row: Models.Document & Todo) {
    if (event.endsWith('.create')) {
        console.log('➕ New row created:', row.$id, row.title);
        // e.g. append to UI list
    } else if (event.endsWith('.update')) {
        console.log('✏️ Row updated:', row.$id, 'done =', row.done);
        // e.g. patch existing item in state
    } else if (event.endsWith('.delete')) {
        console.log('🗑️ Row deleted:', row.$id);
        // e.g. remove from UI list
    }
}

// --- File event handler ---
function handleFileEvent(event: string, file: Models.File) {
    if (event.endsWith('.create')) {
        console.log('📤 File uploaded:', file.name, `(${file.sizeOriginal} bytes)`);
        // e.g. show upload notification / refresh gallery
    } else if (event.endsWith('.update')) {
        console.log('📝 File metadata updated:', file.$id);
    } else if (event.endsWith('.delete')) {
        console.log('🗑️ File removed:', file.name);
    }
}

// Later, when you want to stop listening (e.g. component unmount):
// await subscription.close();
```

### Key points

- **One subscription, multiple channels** — passing an array of channels to `realtime.subscribe()` multiplexes both event streams over a single WebSocket connection.
- **Event routing** — `response.events` contains strings like `tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.create` or `buckets.*.files.*.create`. Checking the prefix (`tablesdb.` vs `buckets.`) tells you the resource type, and the suffix (`.create` / `.update` / `.delete`) tells you the action.
- **Typed payloads** — cast `response.payload` to `Models.Document` (plus your row interface) for rows, or `Models.File` for uploads.
- **Cleanup** — `subscription.close()` unsubscribes; call it when the listener is no longer needed (e.g., in a React `useEffect` cleanup).
- **Permissions matter** — the client only receives realtime events for rows/files it has `read` permission on. If events aren't arriving, check table/bucket and row/file-level permissions first.

Pass an array of channels to a single `subscribe()` call:

```typescript
import { Realtime, Channel } from 'appwrite';

const realtime = new Realtime(client);

const subscription = await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        Channel.tablesdb('[DATABASE_ID]').table('[TABLE_ID]').row(),
        Channel.bucket('[BUCKET_ID]').file(),
    ],
    (response) => {
        console.log(response.events);    // e.g. ['tablesdb.*.tables.*.rows.*.create']
        console.log(response.channels);  // which channel(s) matched
        console.log(response.payload);   // the affected row or file
    }
);
```

The exact channel strings these helpers resolve to are:

| Channel string | Listens to |
|---|---|
| `tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows` | All row events (create/update/delete) in the table |
| `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files` | All file events (create/update/delete) in the bucket |

You can also write them as raw strings if you prefer:

```typescript
await realtime.subscribe(
    [
        'tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows',
        'buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files',
    ],
    (response) => { /* ... */ }
);
```

Notes:
- A single callback receives events from **both** channels — use `response.events` or `response.channels` to distinguish whether the payload is a row or a file.
- To target one specific resource instead of all, append the ID: `tablesdb.[DATABASE_ID].tables.[TABLE_ID].rows.[ROW_ID]` or `buckets.[BUCKET_ID].files.[FILE_ID]`.
- Clean up with `await subscription.close()` when done.

Here's a complete email/password auth implementation with the Appwrite Web SDK.

## Installation

```bash
npm install appwrite
```

## 1. Client Setup

Create a single shared client instance (`src/lib/appwrite.ts`):

```typescript
import { Client, Account, ID, AppwriteException } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // e.g. https://nyc.cloud.appwrite.io/v1
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

export const account = new Account(client);
export { client, ID, AppwriteException };
```

> Replace `<REGION>` with your project's region (`nyc`, `fra`, `syd`, ...). For self-hosted, use `https://your-domain/v1`. Your project must have a **Web platform** registered with your app's hostname, or requests will be blocked by CORS.

## 2. Signup (create account + auto-login)

`account.create()` only creates the user — it does **not** log them in. You must follow it with `createEmailPasswordSession()`.

```typescript
import { account, ID } from './lib/appwrite';

export async function signUp(email: string, password: string, name: string) {
    // 1. Create the user account
    await account.create({
        userId: ID.unique(),
        email,
        password,   // min 8 characters
        name,
    });

    // 2. Log them in immediately — creates a session cookie
    await account.createEmailPasswordSession({ email, password });

    // 3. Return the now-authenticated user
    return await account.get();
}
```

## 3. Login

```typescript
export async function logIn(email: string, password: string) {
    const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({ email, password });
    console.log(session.$id, session.expire);
    return await account.get();
}
```

## 4. Get current user / check auth state

```typescript
export async function getCurrentUser() {
    try {
        return await account.get();   // throws 401 if no valid session
    } catch {
        return null;
    }
}
```

## 5. Logout

```typescript
export async function logOut() {
    await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
}
```

## 6. Error handling

```typescript
import { account, ID, AppwriteException } from './lib/appwrite';

async function handleSignUp(email: string, password: string, name: string) {
    try {
        await account.create({ userId: ID.unique(), email, password, name });
        await account.createEmailPasswordSession({ email, password });
        return await account.get();
    } catch (err) {
        if (err instanceof AppwriteException) {
            switch (err.type) {
                case 'user_already_exists':
                    throw new Error('An account with that email already exists.');
                case 'password_personal_data':
                    throw new Error('Password must not contain personal data.');
                case 'general_argument_invalid':
                    throw new Error('Invalid email or password format.');
                default:
                    // err.code → 401, 409, 429, etc.
                    throw new Error(err.message);
            }
        }
        throw err;
    }
}
```

## 7. React example wiring it together

```tsx
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { account, ID } from './lib/appwrite';
import type { Models } from 'appwrite';

export function useAuth() {
    const [user, setUser] = useState<Models.User<Models.Preferences> | null>(null);
    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

    useEffect(() => {
        account.get()
            .then(setUser)
            .catch(() => setUser(null))
            .finally(() => setLoading(false));
    }, []);

    const login = async (email: string, password: string) => {
        await account.createEmailPasswordSession({ email, password });
        setUser(await account.get());
    };

    const register = async (email: string, password: string, name: string) => {
        await account.create({ userId: ID.unique(), email, password, name });
        await login(email, password);
    };

    const logout = async () => {
        await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
        setUser(null);
    };

    return { user, loading, login, register, logout };
}
```

## Key notes

| Point | Detail |
|---|---|
| Session storage | The Web SDK stores the session in a secure cookie automatically — no manual token handling needed. All subsequent SDK calls on the same `client` are authenticated. |
| `ID.unique()` | Lets Appwrite generate the user ID. You can pass your own custom ID string instead (must be valid: max 36 chars, alphanumeric + `.-_`, can't start with a special char). |
| Password rules | Minimum 8 characters. Appwrite hashes with Argon2 server-side. |
| Existing session | Calling `createEmailPasswordSession()` while already logged in throws `user_session_already_exists` (401). Delete the current session first if you need to switch accounts. |
| SSR frameworks | For Next.js / SvelteKit / Nuxt server-side auth, use `node-appwrite` with an admin client + `a_session_<PROJECT_ID>` cookie instead of this browser flow. |

## What a session is

A session is a server-side record that proves a user authenticated. Every session has an `$id`, a `secret`, an `expire` timestamp, the provider that created it (`email`, `oauth2`, `anonymous`, `magic-url`, `phone`, `token`…), device/IP/geo metadata, and a `factors` array recording which auth factors were satisfied (used by MFA).

Users can hold **many** sessions at once — one per device/browser — and each is independently revocable.

---

## Creating sessions

All session-creation paths live on the `Account` service. The client SDK stores the resulting session automatically (cookie on web, secure storage on React Native/Flutter).

```typescript
import { Client, Account, OAuthProvider, ID } from 'appwrite';

const account = new Account(client);

// 1. Email + password
await account.createEmailPasswordSession({ email, password });

// 2. Anonymous (upgradeable later via updateEmail/updatePassword)
await account.createAnonymousSession();

// 3. OAuth2 — browser redirect flow, session created on return
account.createOAuth2Session({
    provider: OAuthProvider.Github,
    success: 'https://example.com/success',
    failure: 'https://example.com/fail',
    scopes: ['repo'],
});
```

### Two-step "token" flows

Magic URL, email OTP, phone OTP, OAuth-on-mobile, and server-generated custom tokens all produce a **token** (`userId` + `secret`) which you then exchange for a session:

```typescript
// Magic URL / email OTP / phone OTP
await account.createMagicURLToken({ userId: ID.unique(), email, url: 'https://example.com/verify' });
// …user clicks link / enters code, then:
await account.createSession({ userId, secret });
```

This is the **only** OAuth path that works on React Native — `createOAuth2Session()` is web-only:

```typescript
const loginUrl = await account.createOAuth2Token({ provider, success: deepLink, failure: deepLink });
const result = await WebBrowser.openAuthSessionAsync(`${loginUrl}`, scheme);
const url = new URL(result.url);
await account.createSession({
    userId: url.searchParams.get('userId'),
    secret: url.searchParams.get('secret'),
});
```

### SSR (Next.js, SvelteKit, Nuxt, Remix, Astro)

Server-rendered apps need two clients: an **admin client** (API key, reusable singleton) to *create* sessions, and a **session client** (per-request, never shared) to *act as* the user. Only an admin client receives `session.secret`.

```typescript
import { Client, Account } from 'node-appwrite';

const account = new Account(adminClient);
const session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession({ email, password });

// Cookie name MUST be a_session_<PROJECT_ID>
res.cookie('a_session_[PROJECT_ID]', session.secret, {
    httpOnly: true, secure: true, sameSite: 'strict',
    expires: new Date(session.expire), path: '/',
});
```

Then per request:

```typescript
const sessionClient = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .setSession(req.cookies['a_session_[PROJECT_ID]'])
    .setForwardedUserAgent(req.headers['user-agent']); // records the real end-user device

const user = await new Account(sessionClient).get();
```

---

## Managing sessions

```typescript
await account.get();                                  // current user (401 if no valid session)
await account.listSessions();                         // all sessions for this user
await account.getSession({ sessionId: 'current' });   // 'current' is a valid alias everywhere
await account.updateSession({ sessionId: 'current' }); // extend expiry + refresh OAuth provider tokens
```

`updateSession()` is what you call to keep long-lived sessions alive and to renew an expired `providerAccessToken` using the stored refresh token.

For short-lived, forwardable credentials (e.g. calling an Appwrite Function or your own backend as the user), issue a **JWT** instead of passing the session secret:

```typescript
const { jwt } = await account.createJWT();  // ~15 min lifetime
serverClient.setJWT(jwt);
```

Server-side admin management uses the `Users` service: `users.listSessions()`, `users.deleteSession()`, `users.deleteSessions()`.

---

## Deleting sessions

```typescript
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });  // logout this device
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: '[ID]' });     // revoke one specific device
await account.deleteSessions();                         // logout everywhere, incl. current
```

Deleting a session invalidates its secret immediately. In SSR, also clear the cookie — the browser doesn't know the server-side record is gone. Note that JWTs are *not* revoked by session deletion until they expire, so keep their lifetime short.

---

## Session limits & length

Both are **project-level settings** (Console → Auth → Security), not per-call options:

| Setting | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **Session limit** | 10 | Max concurrent sessions per user. Configurable up to 100. When a user exceeds it, Appwrite **automatically deletes the oldest session** — new logins never fail because of this. |
| **Session length** | 1 year | Max expiry for a new session; can be shortened (e.g. 1 hour for banking-style apps). `updateSession()` resets the clock. |

Other limits worth knowing:

- **Rate limits** — session endpoints are aggressively throttled (typically 10 requests per IP per 15 min per email/method). Expect `429` on brute-force. An admin client with an API key bypasses these, which is exactly why SSR logins go through the admin client.
- **MFA** — if MFA is enabled, `createEmailPasswordSession()` returns a session that is only *partially* valid; requests fail with `401 user_more_factors_required` until you complete a challenge. The satisfied factors are then reflected in `session.factors` / `mfaUpdatedAt`.
- **Cross-domain cookies** — third-party cookie blocking breaks the default cookie flow when your app and Appwrite are on different domains. Fixes are a custom domain (`api.yourapp.com`) or explicitly passing the secret via `client.setSession()`.

Standard error handling applies — catch `AppwriteException` and branch on `err.type` (`user_invalid_credentials`, `user_session_already_exists`, `user_more_factors_required`) rather than just the code.

## Important caveat first

Creating databases, tables, and columns are **server-side operations** — they require an API key, which must never ship in browser code. The Web SDK (`appwrite`) can only do the row operations. So this is a two-part answer:

- **Setup script** (`node-appwrite`, run once from your machine/CI) → database + table + columns
- **Browser code** (`appwrite`) → insert the row

Also note `Databases` / `createDocument` are deprecated — `TablesDB` / `createRow` is the current API.

---

## 1. Setup script — `node-appwrite`

```typescript
// scripts/setup.ts  — run with an API key, never in the browser
import { Client, TablesDB, Permission, Role } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID!)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY!);

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

// Use stable IDs, not ID.unique(), so the script is re-runnable and
// the IDs can be hardcoded in your client app.
const DATABASE_ID = 'main';
const TABLE_ID = 'tasks';

// 1. Database
await tablesDB.create({
    databaseId: DATABASE_ID,
    name: 'Main',
});

// 2. Table + columns in one call
await tablesDB.createTable({
    databaseId: DATABASE_ID,
    tableId: TABLE_ID,
    name: 'tasks',
    columns: [
        { key: 'title',     type: 'varchar', size: 255, required: true },
        { key: 'completed', type: 'boolean',            required: false },
        { key: 'dueDate',   type: 'datetime',           required: false },
    ],
    // Table-level permissions: any signed-in user can create,
    // and per-row permissions (set below) govern read/update/delete.
    permissions: [
        Permission.create(Role.users()),
    ],
    rowSecurity: true,
});

console.log('Done:', DATABASE_ID, TABLE_ID);
```

Notes on the column types:
- `varchar` is stored inline in the row and is **fully indexable when `size` ≤ 768** — right choice for a short title. The legacy bare `string` type is deprecated.
- For long free-text (a task description, say) use `text` / `mediumtext` / `longtext` instead — those live off-page, don't eat the 64 KB row budget, and don't need `size`, but only support prefix indexes.
- If you prefer creating columns individually (e.g. adding one later), there are dedicated methods — `createVarcharColumn`, `createBooleanColumn`, `createDatetimeColumn`. Check `appwrite tablesdb create-varchar-column --help` or the API reference for their exact parameters rather than guessing.

